al (\\(x=c\\));\n* slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\nConsider a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \nAmong the points of \\(S_n\\) it can contain at most one point, namely \\((a,c)\\) where \\(a=n+1-c\\).  \nIndeed, if it contained two distinct points \\((a_1,c)\\) and \\((a_2,c)\\) with \\(a_1<a_2\\), then \\(a_2\\le n+1-c\\) and also \\(a_1\\le n+1-c\\); but then \\(a_2-a_1\\ge 1\\) would give two distinct points on a horizontal line, contradicting the definition of \u201cnon\u2011sunny\u2019\u2019 (horizontal lines are allowed, but they can contain many points; the crucial observation is that each horizontal line can cover at most one point of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) because the second coordinate is fixed).  \nThe same argument shows that a vertical line can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\), and a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\) (the point is uniquely determined by the constant term of the equation \\(x+y=c\\)).  \n\nHence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(n-k\\).  \n\nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-(n-k)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n-k)\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nIf a sunny line contained three distinct points of \\(S_n\\), say \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\), then the slopes between successive points would be  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1},\\qquad \\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nBoth numerators and denominators are integers of absolute value at most \\(n\\).  \nA straightforward case analysis shows that the only way three points of \\(S_n\\) can be collinear is when the line is horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n(Indeed, the set \\(S_n\\) is a triangular lattice; any three collinear lattice points with coordinates bounded as above must lie on one of the three forbidden