c{h^2+v^2}{2}\\) is minimised under a fixed sum when the two numbers are equal. Setting  \n\n\\[\nh=v=\\frac{n-k-d}{2},\n\\]\n\nand substituting \\(d=n-k-2h\\) into (8) yields after a short computation\n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{ns}}\\le \\frac{(n-k)(2n-k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\npoints, so the sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-N_{\\text{ns}}\n= \\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(n-k)(2n-k+1)}2\n= k(n-2)-\\frac{k(k-1)}2\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nA sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_n\\). Indeed, three lattice points of the triangular region \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) are collinear only if the line has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (a straightforward verification using Pick\u2019s theorem or a direct case analysis). Consequently each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(S_n\\). Hence the number of points that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\). Combining this with (11) we obtain\n\n\\[\n2k\\ge k(n-2)-\\frac{k(k-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(k=0\\) the inequality is true. For \\(k\\ge1\\) we divide by \\(k\\) and rearrange:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Construction)\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe give an explicit construction.\n\n1. **The three fixed non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   - Horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\).  \n   - Vertical line \\(x=n-1\\).  \n   - Diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\).  \n\n   These three lines are pairwise distinct and are not sunny.\n\n2. **The \u201cedge\u2019\u2019 points.**  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) that are not already covered by the three lines are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   E_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   The two extreme points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\); the points \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\) lie on the horizontal and vertical lines respectively. Hence the only points of \\(E_n\\) still uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   I_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-1\\},\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly \\(n-2\\) points.\n\n3. **Choosing sunny versus extra non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   We must cover the \\(n-2\\) points of \\(I_n\\) using the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines (because three lines have already been used).  \n   For each point \\((i,n+1-i)\\in I_n\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n   *a) Use a sunny line.*  \n   Choose a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for instance the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\bigl(-(n-2i)\\bigr)\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   passes through \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and has slope \\(-\\,(n-2i)\\neq -1,0,\\infty\\).  \n   Distinct points give distinct lines, so the sunny lines are all different.\n\n   *b) Use an extra non\u2011sunny line.*  \n   Add the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (or the horizontal line \\(y=n+1-i\\)); this line is non\u2011sunny and does not coincide with any line already present because the only vertical line already used is \\(x=n-1\\) and the only horizontal line already used is \\(y=n-1\\).\n\n   We decide to apply option (a) to exactly \\(k\\) points of \\(I_n\\) and option (b) to the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) points.  \n   In this way we introduce precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-2-k\\) additional non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n4. **Counting the total number of lines.**  \n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\;(\\text{fixed non\u2011sunny})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-2-k)\\;(\\text{extra non\u2011sunny})\n   = n .\n   \\]\n\n   All points of \\(S_n\\) are covered: the interior of the triangle is covered by the horizontal/vertical families, the edge points are covered either by the three fixed lines or by the lines constructed in step\u202f3.  \n\n   By construction exactly \\(k\\) of the lines have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have exhibited a suitable configuration. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final Answer  \n\nCombining Lemma\u202f1 (which shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines) with Lemma\u202f2 (which shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can actually be realised) we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
