            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nWe must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and prove that they cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1 Sunny diagonal lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_i:\\; y = x-(i-1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(D_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore each \\(D_i\\) is sunny.  \nBecause the intercepts \\(-(i-1)\\) are distinct, the lines \\(D_1,\\dots ,D_k\\) are distinct.\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\) lies on some \\(D_i\\) **iff**\n\n\\[\na-b = i-1\\quad\\text{for some }i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. **iff**\n\n\\[\na-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2 Non‑sunny lines for the remaining points  \n\nAssume that \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) does **not** satisfy (3).  \nThen either  \n\n\\[\na-b\\le -1\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad a-b\\ge k . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(a-b\\ge k\\)*, then \\(a\\ge k+1\\).  \nWe cover all such points by the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\; x=a . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(a-b\\le -1\\)*, then \\(b-a\\ge1\\) and consequently \\(b\\ge k+1\\) (otherwise \\(b\\le k\\) would force \\(a\\le k\\) and then \\(a-b\\ge -(k-1)\\), contradicting \\(a-b\\le -1\\)).  \nWe cover all such points by the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\; y=b . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3 Choosing a family that respects the line budget  \n\nThe two families \\(\\{V_a\\mid a=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(\\{H_b\\mid b=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) each contain exactly \\(n-k\\) lines.  \nWe keep **one** of them; the other is discarded.  \nThus the total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{sunny diagonals})\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;(\\text{chosen non‑sunny lines})\\;=\\;n . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct: the diagonals have slope \\(1\\) while the kept vertical (resp. horizontal) lines have slope \\(\\infty\\) (resp. \\(0\\)), and within each family the defining constants differ.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4 Verification of the covering condition  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then (3) holds, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{a-b+1}\\).\n\n* If \\(a-b\\ge k\\) then \\(a\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which we keep when we chose the vertical family).\n\n* If \\(a-b\\le -1\\) then \\(b\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (which we keep when we chose the horizontal family).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Completeness of the description of admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a concrete configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nTogether with the trivial bound (1) this proves that the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Final answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
