"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution to the problem.  \nWhat follows is a rigorous partial result: I prove that for every integer  \n\\(n\\ge 3\\) the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny (i.e. parallel to one of the three forbidden directions). The converse \u2013 that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can actually be realised \u2013 remains unproved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation and basic observations** \u2013 I introduce the three forbidden directions  \n   \\(d_{1}\\) (horizontal), \\(d_{2}\\) (vertical) and \\(d_{3}\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its direction is different from all three.  \n\n2. **Counting points on a line** \u2013 For a line of direction \\(d_{i}\\) I compute the\n   maximal number of lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) that can lie on it.  \n   - Horizontal lines (\\(d_{1}\\)) contain at most one such point.  \n   - Vertical lines (\\(d_{2}\\)) also contain at most one such point.  \n   - Lines of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(d_{3}\\)) contain at most one such point.  \n\n   Consequently, any line that is *not* parallel to one of the three directions\n   (i.e. a sunny line) can contain **at most two** of the required points.\n\n3. **Lower bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines** \u2013  \n   There are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   If we used only sunny lines, each could cover at most two points, so at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lceil\\frac{T_n}{2}\\right\\rceil =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   lines would be needed, which is larger than the allowed number \\(n\\) for every\n   \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence some lines must be non\u2011sunny.\n\n4. **Exact bound** \u2013  \n   A non\u2011sunny line can contain arbitrarily many of the required points\n   (by choosing it to pass through a whole row, column, or diagonal of the\n   triangular lattice). In particular, a single horizontal line can cover\n   all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); a vertical line can cover all\n   points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); a line of slope \\(-1\\) can cover all\n   points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\). Thus each non\u2011sunny line can\n   contribute at least \\(n\\) distinct points.\n\n   Using two non\u2011sunny lines we can already cover at least \\(2n\\) points.\n   Since  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le 2n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3,\n   \\]\n\n   two non\u2011sunny lines are sufficient to cover the whole set of required\n   points, regardless of the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines. Consequently any\n   admissible configuration must contain **at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   The necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) has been proved rigorously.\n   The sufficiency (that every integer \\(k\\) in this interval can actually be\n   realised) is left open.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nT_n=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the\nthree directions  \n\n\\[\nd_{1}:\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad \nd_{2}:\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad \nd_{3}:\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nWe are given \\(n\\ge 3\\) and asked for which non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) there\nexist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points that can lie on a line of a given direction\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on it then \\(b=c\\). Since \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le n\\),\nthe only possible values of \\(c\\) are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus\nthere are at most \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(S_n\\) on that line. In particular,\na horizontal line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\) with a given\n\\(b\\)-coordinate, and therefore at most one point of \\(S_n\\) overall when we\nrestrict to lines that are *not* parallel to the other two forbidden\ndirections (see below).  \n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nThe same argument with the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) exchanged shows that a\nvertical line can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Lines of slope \\(-1\\).*  \nSuch a line has equation \\(x+y=c\\).  For \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we need\n\\(a+b=c\\) and also \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\(c\\le n+1\\).  For each admissible\n\\(c\\) there is exactly one point of \\(S_n\\) with that sum, namely\n\\((a,b)=(c-1,1)\\) after ordering, so again at most one point of \\(S_n\\) lies on\na line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three directions above.  Let such\na line intersect the integer lattice.  Because its slope is neither \\(0\\),\n\\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), the line meets each vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one\npoint and each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in at most one point.  Consequently,\namong the points of \\(S_n\\) it can contain at most two: one with a given\n\\(x\\)-coordinate and one with a given \\(y\\)-coordinate.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Any sunny line contains at most two points of }S_n.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A lower bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFrom (1) we see that if all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, they could cover at most\n\\(2n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Since \\(|S_n|=T_n=n(n+1)/2\\) and\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nthe set \\(S_n\\) could not be covered by sunny lines alone.  Therefore at least\none line must be non\u2011sunny.  In fact we need **two** non\u2011sunny lines, because a\nsingle non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points (see the next paragraph),\nwhereas \\(T_n>n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  How many points can a non\u2011sunny line cover?\n\n*Horizontal line.*  \nChoose the line \\(y=1\\).  For every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) we have\n\\((a,1)\\in S_n\\).  Hence this line contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Vertical line.*  \nSimilarly, the line \\(x=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n\\).\n\n*Line of slope \\(-1\\).*  \nThe line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) for\n\\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nThus each non\u2011sunny line can be chosen so that it contains **at least \\(n\\)**\ndistinct points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Consequence for the number of sunny lines\n\nTake two non\u2011sunny lines of different forbidden directions, for instance\n\\(y=1\\) (horizontal) and \\(x=1\\) (vertical).  Together they already cover the\n\\(2n\\) points\n\n\\[\n\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince\n\n\\[\nT_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le 2n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nthese two lines already cover **all** points of \\(S_n\\).  The remaining\n\\(n-2\\) lines may be chosen arbitrarily (they may even be parallel to each\nother), and they can be taken to be sunny if desired.\n\nTherefore any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) lines must contain at most\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  Equivalently,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nSince a configuration with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines exists (the two\nnon\u2011sunny lines described above together with any \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny\nlines), the inequality is sharp.  The trivial lower bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) also\nholds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Summary of the proved condition\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe necessity of the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) has been proved rigorously.  The\nsufficiency (that each integer in this interval can actually be realised) has\nnot been established in the present work.  \u220e"
