er the set  \n\n\\[\nC_k=\\{\\,2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}\\setminus\\{\\,i+1\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(C_k\\) is \\((n)-k\\).  \nFor each \\(c\\in C_k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nM_c:\\; x+y=c .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(M_c\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non‑sunny.  \nThe constants \\(c\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(\\{M_c\\mid c\\in C_k\\}\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nLet \\(d=a-b\\). There are two possibilities.\n\n*Case 1.*  \\(d=i-1\\) for some \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \nThen \\(b=a-i+1\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (1) of \\(L_i\\); hence it lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\n*Case 2.*  \\(d\\neq i-1\\) for all \\(i\\le k\\).  \nThen the integer \\(c:=a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) but is **not** of the form \\(i+1\\) with \\(i\\le k\\); consequently \\(c\\in C_k\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=c\\) and lies on the non‑sunny line \\(M_c\\).\n\nIn either case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines. Hence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{L_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\cup\\{M_c\\mid c\\in C_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Counting the lines and sunny lines\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{sunny lines})\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;(\\text{non‑sunny lines})\\;=\\;n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, because the slopes of the two subfamilies are different (1 versus \\(-1\\)).  \nTherefore \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies both requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nTogether with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\), this shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
