most that many points of\n   \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set \\(T_n\\) has  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   For each possible sum \\(s=a+b\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are exactly\n   \\(s-1\\) ordered pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\).  Summing gives  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\qquad\\Box\n   \\]\n\n   *Lemma 3.*  \n   When \\(n\\) lines are all sunny they cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points.  Hence the total number of\n   *distinct* points that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Compare (2) with (1).\n\n   * If \\(n=2m\\) is even, then \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=m\\) and\n     \\(n\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor =2m^{2}<m(2m+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n   * If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd, then \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=m+1\\) and\n     \\(n\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor =(2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n     Equality would require **every** sunny line to contain exactly\n     \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  The only sunny lines attaining \\(m+1\\) points are\n     those of slope \\(1\\) passing through the origin, i.e. the single line\n     \\(y=x\\).  Since we have \\(n\\ge3\\) distinct lines, at least one line contains\n     at most \\(m\\) points, making the total strictly smaller than\n     \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n   In all cases the bound (2) is strictly less than \\(|T_n|\\); therefore\n   \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n   Lemma\u202f3 shows that the case \\(k=n\\) never occurs.\n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\))**\n\n   Fix such a \\(