he\n   point \\((1,1)\\); there is exactly one such line, namely \\(y=x\\).  Thus we could\n   have at most one sunny line with the maximal size, contradicting the assumption\n   that \\(n-1\\ge2\\) sunny lines exist.  Therefore at least two lines must be\n   non‑sunny. ∎\n\n   Lemma 3 immediately yields the **upper bound**\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)**  \n\n   Fix such a \\(k\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny,\n   that cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – two non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Take the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  Both are\n   non‑sunny and together contain every point of the first row and the first column\n   of the triangle \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining \\(n-2\\) columns.*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\; y = x - c + 1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   The line \\(L_c\\) has slope \\(1\\); hence it is sunny.  It passes through the lattice\n   points\n   \\[\n   (c,1),\\;(c+1,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(n,\\,n-c+1\\bigr),\n   \\]\n   i.e. through the whole diagonal segment of column \\(x=c\\) that lies inside\n   \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – covering all points.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   - If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n   - If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n   - If \\(a\\ge2\\) then \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently\n     \\(b\\le n-a+1\\).  The point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(L_a\\) because\n     \\(b = a-a+1 = 1\\) when \\(b=1\\) (already handled) or, for \\(b>1\\),\n     we have \\(b = a - a + 1 + (b-1) = a - a + 1 + (b-1)\\) and the definition of\n     \\(L_a\\) gives exactly the points \\((a,1), (a+1,2),\\dots\\).  More directly,\n     solving \\(b = x - a + 1\\) for \\(x\\) yields \\(x = a + b -1\\); since\n     \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(x\\le n\\) and \\(x\\ge a\\), so the point indeed lies on\n     the segment of \\(L_a\\) that is inside the triangle.  Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   *Step 4 – counting sunny lines.*  \n   Among the \\(n\\) lines we have the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); the\n   remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are the sunny lines \\(L_c\\) with \\(c=2,\\dots ,n\\).\n   To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we simply keep the first \\(k\\) of the\n   \\(L_c\\)’s and replace the other \\(n-2-k\\) of them by any non‑sunny lines that\n   are already present (for instance, duplicate the vertical line \\(x=1\\) after a\n   slight translation, e.g. \\(x=2\\), which is also non‑sunny).  Because the two\n   original non‑sunny lines already cover the whole first row and column, adding\n   further non‑sunny vertical lines does not destroy coverage.  Consequently we\n   obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that\n   covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n   This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n\n   * The inequality (5) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).  \n   * The explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with\n     \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
