rbidden slopes guarantee \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\):  \n   \u2013 if \\(p=0\\) then \\(m=0\\) (horizontal), excluded;  \n   \u2013 if \\(p=-q\\) then \\(m=-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), excluded;  \n   \u2013 otherwise \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).\n\n   Inequality (3) shows that the integer parameter \\(t\\) can take at most\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(x_0+y_0)}{|p+q|}\\big\\rfloor\\) different values.\n   Since \\(x_0+y_0\\ge 2\\), the right\u2011hand side is bounded by\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).\n   Adding the initial point (\\(t=0\\)) we obtain that the line contains at most\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n4. **A necessary number of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f3.*  \n   In any family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) at least two lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Assume, to the contrary, that at most one line is non\u2011sunny.  Then at least\n   \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny, and by Lemma\u202f1 each of them covers at most\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Hence the total number\n   of points that can be covered is at most\n   \\[\n   (n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   =n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) (Lemma\u202f2), equality in (4) would be required.\n   Equality forces \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), i.e. \\(n\\) is\n   odd, and every sunny line must attain the maximal possible size\n   \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).\n\n   Lemma\u202f1 shows that a sunny line attains the bound \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) **iff**\n   \\(|p+q|=2\\).  The coprime condition \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) leaves only the possibilities\n   \\((p,q)=(1,1)\\) or \\((p,q)=(-1,-1)\\); the latter gives slope \\(-1\\), which is\n   forbidden.  Thus the only sunny lines that can contain \\