horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n   - If \\(a\\ge 2\\) then \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently\n     \\(b\\le n-a+1\\).  The point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(L_a\\) because,\n     by (7) with \\(c=a\\), the line \\(L_a\\) contains precisely the points\n     \\((a,1),(a+1,2),\\dots ,(a+b-1,b)\\).  Since \\(b\\le n-a+1\\), the index\n     \\(a+b-1\\) does not exceed \\(n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) indeed appears in that list.\n     Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 adjusting the number of sunny lines.*  \n   The construction above uses exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(L_c\\) with\n   \\(c=2,\\dots ,n\\)).  To obtain a family with **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines we\n   replace \\(n-2-k\\) of the lines \\(L_c\\) by additional non\u2011sunny lines.\n   For each replacement we may take a vertical line \\(x=t\\) with a new integer\n   \\(t\\) (different from the already used \\(x=1\\)) or a horizontal line\n   \\(y=s\\) with a new integer \\(s\\neq1\\); both are non\u2011sunny and do not affect the\n   covering property because the points of the first row and first column are already\n   covered by \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  The resulting family consists of\n\n   * the two original non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\);\n   * exactly \\(k\\) of the sunny lines \\(L_c\\);\n   * \\(n-2-k\\) new non\u2011sunny vertical (or horizontal) lines.\n\n   All lines are distinct, the total number of lines is \\(n\\), and precisely \\(k\\)\n   of them are sunny.  The covering argument of Step\u202f3 remains valid because the\n   points that were previously covered by a removed sunny line \\(L_c\\) are still\n   covered: each such point \\((c,1)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=c\\) that we have\n   introduced as a replacement, and every other point of \\(L_c\\) has the same first\n   coordinate \\(c\\) and therefore also lies on that vertical line.  Hence th