er bound**\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   *Base covering with \\(n\\) lines.*  \n   Define the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n   * two non‑sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     X:\\;x=1,\\qquad Y:\\;y=1 ;\n     \\]\n\n   * for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) the line  \n     \\[\n     L_c:\\; y = x - c + 1 .\n     \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n     The slope of \\(L_c\\) is \\(1\\); therefore every \\(L_c\\) is sunny.\n\n   There are exactly \\(2+(n-1)=n\\) lines.  We first show that they cover all points\n   of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   **Coverage proof.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(X\\).\n   * If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(Y\\).\n   * Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  Set \\(c=a-b+1\\).  \n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); the case \\(c=1\\) would give\n     \\(a=b\\), which is impossible here since both coordinates are at least \\(2\\).\n     Hence \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) and the line \\(L_c\\) is defined.  Substituting \\(c\\) into\n     (6) we obtain\n     \\[\n     y = x - (a-b+1) + 1 = x - a + b .\n     \\]\n     Evaluating at \\((a,b)\\) gives \\(b = a - a + b\\), i.e. the equality holds, so\n     \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_c\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   *Adjusting the number of sunny lines.*  \n   In the family above the number of sunny lines equals \\(n-2\\) (all the \\(L_c\\)’s).\n   To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we replace \\(n-2-k\\) of the lines\n   \\(L_c\\) by additional non‑sunny lines, keeping the total number of lines equal\n   to \\(n\\).\n\n   For each replacement choose a vertical line \\(x=t\\) with a new integer\n   \\(t\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) that has not been used before.  Such a line is\n   non‑sunny and is distinct from all previously listed lines.  We claim that the\n   covering property is preserved.\n\n   Indeed, suppose we replace \\(L_c\\) by the vertical line \\(x=c\\).  Every point\n   that was covered by \\(L_c\\) has the form \\((c+t,\\,1+t)\\) with\n   \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,n-c\\) (see (6)).  All these points satisfy \\(x\\ge c\\); the\n   particular point \\((c,1)\\) lies on the new vertical line \\(x=c\\), and for\n   \\(t\\ge 1\\) we have \\(x=c+t>c\\), so the point is already covered by one of the\n   other lines \\(L_{c+t}\\) that remain in the family (because we only delete the\n   line \\(L_c\\) itself).  Hence no point loses its covering.\n\n   Performing this replacement exactly \\(n-2-k\\) times yields a family consisting of\n\n   * the two fixed non‑sunny lines \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\);\n   * exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines among the remaining \\(L_c\\)’s;\n   * \\(n-2-k\\) new vertical lines, all non‑sunny.\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(n\\), they are pairwise distinct, and precisely\n   \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  The covering argument above shows that every point of\n   \\(T_n\\) is still covered.\n\n   Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration\n   exists.\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n\n   * The upper bound (5) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\)\n     sunny lines.\n   * The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with\n     \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]"
