um \\(n+2\\).)\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 Complete the covering with \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   For each remaining diagonal sum \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{n+2\\}\\) introduce the line  \n   \\[\n   D_s:\\; x+y=s .\n   \\]\n   These lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore are not sunny.  \n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 Verify the covering.*  \n   - The diagonal lines \\(D_s\\) cover all points whose sum is \\(s\\neq n+2\\).  \n   - The diagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \n     The \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) already pass through the \\(k\\) distinct points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) on this diagonal.  \n     The remaining \\(n+1-(k+1)=n-k\\) points of the same diagonal are covered by the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(D_s\\) (each of those lines meets the diagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\) in exactly one point).  \n\n   Hence every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); together with the trivial impossibility of having more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, this proves that the admissible set of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must be covered.  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\n\\]\n\nthe *diagonal* of sum \\(s\\).  The sets \\(\\Delta_s\\) partition \\(T_n\\) and \\(|\\Delta_s|=s-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nA line that is **sunny** is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nIf a line contains two distinct points of the same diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\), then those two points have coordinates \\((a,b)\\) and \\((c,d)\\) with \\(a+b=c+d=s\\).  The slope of the line through them is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{d-b}{c-a}= \\frac{(s-c)-(s-a)}{c-a}= \\frac{a-c}{c-a}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nThus any line that meets a diagonal in at least two points is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **not sunny**.  \nConsequently a sunny line meets each diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\) in **at most one** point.\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals (\\(s=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)).  Hence a sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  With \\(n\\) lines we can cover at most \\(n\\cdot n\\) points, while \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n\\cdot n\\).  Therefore the covering condition never forces a line to be non\u2011sunny, and the number of sunny lines can never exceed the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_s\\) contains precisely the diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\); the lines are distinct and are all parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), hence none of them is sunny.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the line corresponding to its sum, so the covering condition is satisfied.  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) in the indicated range.\n\n#### 3.1.  Sunny lines\n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_k\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+2-i)\\in\\Delta_{\\,n+2}.\n\\]\n\n(The diagonal \\(\\Delta_{\\,n+2}\\) exists because \\(n+2\\le n+1\\) is false; however we will use it only as a convenient \u201cextra\u201d diagonal that lies outside \\(T_n\\).  The point \\(P_i\\) is nevertheless well defined and will be used to fix the sunny lines.)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=m_i x + b_i ,\\qquad\\text{where }b_i\\text{ is chosen so that }(i,n+2-i)\\in S_i .\n\\]\n\nExplicitly, \\(b_i=n+2-i-m_i i\\).  Each \\(S_i\\) is a line with slope \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  Moreover the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\nEach sunny line \\(S_i\\) meets the diagonal \\(\\Delta_{\\,n+2}\\) in exactly the point \\(P_i\\).\n\n#### 3.2.  Non\u2011sunny (diagonal) lines\n\nFor every sum \\(s\\) with  \n\n\\[\n2\\le s\\le n+1,\\qquad s\\neq n+2,\n\\]\n\nintroduce the line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\; x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and hence are not sunny.  They cover all points of the diagonals \\(\\Delta_s\\) with \\(s\\neq n+2\\).\n\n#### 3.3.  Verification of the covering\n\n* For any \\(s\\neq n+2\\) the whole diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\) is contained in the line \\(D_s\\).\n* The diagonal \\(\\Delta_{\\,n+2}\\) consists of the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \n  Among them the \\(k\\) points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) are already covered by the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  \n  The remaining \\((n+1)-(k+1)=n-k\\) points of \\(\\Delta_{\\,n+2}\\) each lie on a distinct line \\(D_s\\) (because each line \\(D_s\\) meets \\(\\Delta_{\\,n+2}\\) in exactly one point).  \n\nThus every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.  The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* \\(k=0\\) is realizable (the family of diagonal lines).\n* For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists (the construction of \u00a73).\n* No configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines (Lemma of \u00a71).\n\nHenc