tailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\binom{n+2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\nConsequently a **non‑sunny** line is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on a non‑sunny line  \n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=b\\) (\\(b\\ge0\\)).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(0\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence the line\ncontains exactly \\(n+2-b\\le n+2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=a\\) is symmetric and also contains at most \\(n+2\\) points.\n\n*Line of slope \\(-1\\)* \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge0\\)).  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on it satisfy \\(c\\le n+1\\); for a given \\(c\\) the\npoints are \\((0,c),(1,c-1),\\dots ,(c,0)\\), i.e. \\(c+1\\le n+2\\) points.\nThus a line parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\) contains at most \\(n+1\\) points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nHence **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n+2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line; its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\nBecause the three families \\(\\{\\ell_x\\},\\{\\ell_y\\},\\{\\ell_d\\}\\) have distinct\nslopes, \\(L\\) can intersect each family in at most one point.  \nConsequently \\(L\\) can contain at most three points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that lie\non those three families.\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(L\\) contains a fourth point\n\\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  The vectors joining \\((a,b)\\) to any of the three\nalready‑present points are respectively horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).\nIf any of those vectors were parallel to the direction of \\(L