izontal line for each row**.  Any deviation (for instance replacing\n   the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) by a sunny line) would leave at least one point\n   of \\(R_j\\) uncovered, because a sunny line contributes at most one point to\n   that row while the row contains at least two points when \\(j\\le n-2\\).\n\n   Since \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line, at least one row\n   loses its dedicated horizontal line, and that row contains at least two\n   points (the rows \\(R_0,\\dots ,R_{n-2}\\) all have size \\(\\ge2\\)).  Hence that\n   row cannot be completely covered, contradicting the hypothesis that every\n   point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   Therefore no configuration satisfying the covering condition can contain a\n   sunny line; i.e. \\(s=0\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n\n   The construction with the \\(n\\) horizontal lines shows that \\(k=0\\) is\n   attainable.  The argument above proves that any configuration fulfilling the\n   covering requirement must have \\(k=0\\).  Hence the set of admissible values\n   of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k=0}.\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
