with \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{0}=\\{L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal{L}_{0}\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny. This shows that \\(k=0\\) is admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Replacing a diagonal by a sunny line\n\nFix a diagonal \\(L_{c_{0}}\\) (with \\(2\\le c_{0}\\le n+1\\)).\nChoose a point \\(P_{c_{0}}=(1,c_{0}-1)\\in L_{c_{0}}\\) (the left‑most lattice point on that diagonal).\nSelect a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{c_{0}}:\\; y-(c_{0}-1)=\\alpha\\bigl(x-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_{c_{0}}\\) passes through \\(P_{c_{0}}\\) and its slope \\(\\alpha\\) is different from\n\\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)); hence \\(S_{c_{0}}\\) is sunny.\n\nFor any \\(c\\neq c_{0}\\) the diagonal \\(L_{c}\\) has equation \\(y=c-x\\). Solving together with the equation of \\(S_{c_{0}}\\) gives a unique intersection point  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{c-(c_{0}-1)}{1+\\alpha},\\qquad\ny=c-x .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha\\neq -1\\), the denominator is non‑zero, so the intersection exists and is a single point. Thus \\(S_{c_{0}}\\) meets each unchanged diagonal \\(L_{c}\\) in a point that belongs to \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) (the intersection lies on the diagonal, whose points are precisely the required lattice points). Consequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal{L}_{0}\\setminus\\{L_{c_{0}}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_{c_{0}}\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\), and now contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\