(\\ell\\).  As observed above,\n\\[\n\\iota(\\ell)\\le n \\qquad\\text{for every }\\ell\\in\\mathcal L. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSumming over all lines we obtain the total number of incidences\n\\[\nI:=\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\iota(\\ell)\\le n\\cdot n=n^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand each point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is incident to at least one\nline of \\(\\mathcal L\\); therefore\n\\[\nI\\ge |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) and (6) gives\n\\[\nn^{2}\\ge I\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\nor equivalently\n\\[\nn^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\ge0.\n\\]\n\nEquality in (5) can hold only if every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) attains the\nmaximum possible length \\(n\\).  The only lines of length \\(n\\) inside the\ntriangle are precisely the \\(n\\) horizontals \\(\\{H_b\\}\\), the \\(n\\) verticals\n\\(\\{V_a\\}\\) and the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\{D_c\\}\\).  Since \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\), the set\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) must coincide with one of these three families. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence for sunny lines\n\nAssume, contrary to the claim, that a covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\nby \\(n\\) distinct lines contains a sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\text{sunny}}\\).\nThen \\(\\mathcal L\\) cannot contain any of the three families in (2) completely,\nbecause the sunny line is not a member of any of those families and the total\nnumber of lines is exactly \\(n\\).  This contradicts Lemma 2.  Hence **every line\nin any admissible covering must be non‑sunny**, i.e. the number of sunny lines\n\\(k\\) must be\n\n\\[\nk=0. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of a covering with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the whole horizontal family \\(\\{H_b\\}_{b=1}^{n}\\).  These are \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, none of which is sunny, and by construction each point \\((a,b)\\in\n\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(y=b\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(H_b\\).  Thus\nthe conditions of the problem are fulfilled with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nThe only non‑negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\nsatisfying the two stated conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
