left‑most point of row \\(b\\) is \\((1,b)\\); this point is already covered\n   by the horizontal line \\(H_{1}\\) (which belongs to the set (7)).\n\n   Thus each row with index \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) is completely covered by the single\n   sunny line \\(\\ell_{b}\\) together with the already‑present horizontal line\n   \\(H_{1}\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – counting the lines.*  \n   We have used the \\(n-k\\) horizontals (7) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n   \\(\\ell_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n}\\).  All these lines are distinct because\n   they have different slopes (the horizontals have slope \\(0\\) while the\n   \\(\\ell_{b}\\) have distinct positive slopes).  Hence we have produced exactly\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n6. **Verification of coverage**  \n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) there are two cases.\n\n   *If \\(b\\le n-k\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\) from (7).\n\n   *If \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\)*, then either \\(a=n+1-b\\) (the right‑most point of its row)\n   and the point lies on \\(\\ell_{b}\\) by construction, or \\(a<n+1-b\\).  In the\n   latter case the point lies on the same row as \\((n+1-b,b)\\); the line\n   \\(\\ell_{b}\\) contains the whole segment of that row from \\((n+1-b,b)\\) to\n   \\((1,b+1)\\), and the left‑most point \\((1,b)\\) is covered by \\(H_{1}\\).  Hence\n   every point is covered.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n\n   The counting argument shows that no value of \\(k\\) is ruled out, and the\n   explicit construction above works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]   ∎"
