th vertices\n   \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  Any line intersecting this triangle meets it in a (possibly\n   degenerate) segment whose endpoints lie on the boundary of the triangle.\n   The longest possible lattice segment is a side of the triangle; each side\n   contains exactly \\(n\\) lattice points, namely the lines listed in (2).  \n   If the line is not parallel to a side, the segment is strictly shorter, so it\n   contains at most \\(n-1\\) lattice points.  ∎\n\n4. **Incidence counting bound**  \n\n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal\n   P_n\\).  Denote by \\(s\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and by\n   \\(\\iota(\\ell)=|\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\) the number of covered points on a line\n   \\(\\ell\\).  By Lemma 1,\n   \\[\n   \\iota(\\ell)\\le n\\quad\\text{for every }\\ell,\\qquad\n   \\iota(\\ell)\\le n-1\\ \\text{if }\\ell\\text{ is sunny}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Summing (3) over all lines gives\n   \\[\n   I:=\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\iota(\\ell)\\le (n-s)n+s(n-1)=n^{2}-s. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Since each point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be incident to at least one line,\n   \\[\n   I\\ge |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Combining (4) and (5) yields\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-s\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   s\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}2 . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, (6) imposes **no restriction** on\n   \\(s\\); any integer \\(0\\le s\\le n\\) is compatible with the counting bound.\n\n5. **Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)**  \n\n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.*  \n   Take the first \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n   \\[\n   V_{1},V_{2},\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\qquad\\bigl(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\bigr).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   These are non‑sunny and together contain all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose\n   first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – sunny diagonal lines of slope \\(1\\).*  \n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(n-k+1\\le a\\le n\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{a}:\\; y-a+1 = (x-a) . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Equation (8) can be rewritten as \\(y = x - a +1\\); its slope is \\(1\\), hence\n   \\(\\ell_{a}\\) is sunny (slope \\(1\\) is not forbidden).  The line \\(\\ell_{a}\\)\n   passes through the points\n   \\[\n   (a,1),\\;(a+1,2),\\;(a+2,3),\\dots ,\\bigl(n+1-a,\\;n+1-a\\bigr). \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   Observe that for every \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\) the point\n   \\((a+b-1,b)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (because\n   \\((a+b-1)+b = a+b \\le n+1\\)).  Hence **all** points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose\n   first coordinate equals \\(a\\) are contained in \\(\\ell_{a}\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – counting the lines.*  \n   We have used the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (7) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n   \\(\\ell_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n}\\).  All these lines are distinct:\n   the vertical lines have undefined slope, whereas the \\(\\ell_{a}\\) have slope\n   \\(1\\) and different intercepts \\(-a+1\\).  Consequently we have exactly \\(n\\)\n   distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n6. **Verification of coverage**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) from (7).\n\n   *If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\)*, then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) (by the definition of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)),\n   and by (9) the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a}\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by at least one of the\n   constructed lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n\n   The counting argument shows that no value of \\(k\\) is ruled out, and the\n   explicit construction above works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]   ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
