"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n1. **Trivial upper bound:** because we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n2. **Construction for any prescribed \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ):**  \n   * Use \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) (these are not sunny).  \n   * The lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) that are not covered by the horizontals are exactly  \n     \\[\n     S_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\; b\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n     \\]  \n   * For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) draw the line  \n     \\[\n     L_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x + (n+1).\n     \\]  \n     Its slope \\(-\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence each \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \n   * One checks that \\(L_t\\) contains precisely the \\(t\\) points  \n     \\[\n     (1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,\\;(t,n+1-t),\n     \\]  \n     and that the collection \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) covers all points of \\(S_k\\).  \n   * Consequently the total family of \\(n\\) lines (the \\(n-k\\) horizontals together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines) covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n3. **Count of sunny lines:** exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, the remaining \\(n-k\\) horizontals are not. Thus the construction yields a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n4. Since the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are attainable, and no other values are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nWe have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\n#### 2.1 Horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines  \n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\nThese lines cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1,\\qquad 1\\le b\\le n-k .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for any such point we have \\(b=j\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), so the point lies on \\(H_j\\).\n\n#### 2.2 The remaining points\n\nThe points not covered by the horizontals are precisely  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\; b\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(b=n-t+1\\) with \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\bigcup_{t=1}^{k}\\{(a,n-t+1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le t\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|S_k|=\\sum_{t=1}^{k} t=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.3 Sunny lines covering \\(S_k\\)\n\nFor each \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x + (n+1).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Slope.*  The slope of \\(L_t\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{t}{t+1}\\).  \nSince \\(t\\ge 1\\), this number is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) and from \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(L_t\\) is sunny.\n\n*Integer points on \\(L_t\\).*  Substituting an integer \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le t\\) into (7) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{t}{t+1}i + (n+1)\n   = n+1 - \\frac{ti}{t+1}\n   = n+1-i .\n\\]\n\nHence the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,\\;(t,n+1-t)\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nall satisfy (7); i.e. they lie on \\(L_t\\).  \nConversely, if \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\) and \\(a\\) is an integer between\n\\(1\\) and \\(t\\), then \\(b=n+1-a\\), so (8) describes **all** integer points of \\(L_t\\).\n\nComparing (5) and (8) we see that the set of integer points of \\(L_t\\) is exactly the\n\\(t\\) points of \\(S_k\\) having second coordinate \\(b=n-t+1\\).  \nTherefore the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) covers every point of \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification of the whole covering\n\nThe total collection of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}= \\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines (the slopes \\(-\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) are all different, so the \\(L_t\\) are distinct from each other and from the horizontals).\n\nEvery lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs either to a\nhorizontal line (if \\(b\\le n-k\\)) or to one of the sunny lines (if\n\\(b\\ge n-k+1\\)).  Hence the covering condition of the problem is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Number of sunny lines\n\nExactly the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny; the horizontals are not.\nThus the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines fulfilling both requirements of the problem, and\nno larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because there are only \\(n\\) lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
