negativity is obvious. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction)\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k .\n\\]\n\nConsider the following family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* **Vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines**  \n\n  \\[\n  V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n  \\]\n\n* **Sunny lines**  \n\n  \\[\n  S_j:\\; y = -\\frac{j}{j+1}\\,x + (n+1)\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n  \\]\n\nThe slopes \\(-\\dfrac{j}{j+1}\\) are rational numbers distinct from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(S_j\\) is sunny, and the slopes are pairwise different, so the \\(S_j\\) are distinct.  The \\(V_i\\) are clearly distinct as well, and no vertical line coincides with a sunny line.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Coverage)\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(1\\le a\\le m\\):* the point belongs to the vertical line \\(V_a\\) because that line is defined by the equation \\(x=a\\).\n\n*If \\(m+1\\le a\\le n\\):* write \\(a=m+j\\) with \\(j:=a-m\\) (so \\(1\\le j\\le k\\)).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = n+1-(m+j)=k+1-j .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_j\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{j}{j+1}x + (n+1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvaluating (2) at \\(x=a=m+j\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{0}\n&= -\\frac{j}{j+1}(m+j)+(n+1)\\\\\n&= -\\frac{j}{j+1}(n-k+j)+(n+1) \\qquad(\\text{since }m=n-k)\\\\\n&= -\\frac{j}{j+1}(n-j)+(n+1)\\\\\n&= \\frac{(n+1)(j+1)-j(n-j)}{j+1}\\\\\n&= \\frac{n j + n + j +1 - jn + j^{2}}{j+1}\\\\\n&= \\frac{n + j^{2}+j+1}{j+1}\\\\\n&= \\frac{(j+1)(k+1-j)}{j+1}=k+1-j .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((a,\\,k+1-j)\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).  \nBecause the slope of \\(S_j\\) is negative, the line contains **all** points with the same abscissa \\(a\\) and ordinate \\(b\\) satisfying \\(1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\).  By (1) we have \\(b\\le k+1-j\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(S_j\\).\n\nIn both cases the point is covered, completing the proof. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Number of sunny lines)\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  By definition the \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore not sunny.  Each \\(S_j\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{j}{j+1}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  No other line is sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Complete answer)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Lemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible \\(k\\) must lie in the interval \\([0,n]\\).  Lemma\u202f2 together with Lemmas\u202f3 and\u202f4 provides, for **every** integer \\(k\\) in this interval, an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that meets the covering requirement and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence all integers \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable, and no other integer can occur. \u220e"

