{a_{0}}\\setminus\\{ma_{0}+c\\}\\) must be covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines.\n\nEvery remaining line is either\n\n* vertical – it can cover points only with a fixed abscissa, which is not \\(a_{0}\\) by definition of \\(a_{0}\\); or\n* has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal) or \\(-1\\) (the only non‑vertical slopes that can pass through two points having the same abscissa).\n\nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=\\text{const}\\) and therefore cannot contain two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate but different \\(y\\)-coordinates.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(y=-x+s\\); such a line passes through the points of a *single* diagonal \\(a+b=s\\).  Since \\(s\\) is fixed, it contains at most one point with abscissa \\(a_{0}\\).\n\nHence none of the remaining lines can contain any point \\((a_{0},b)\\) with \\(b\\neq ma_{0}+c\\).  \nBecause \\(|B_{a_{0}}|\\ge 2\\) (indeed \\(n+1-a_{0}\\ge 2\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\)), at least one point of \\(B_{a_{0}}\\) is left uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers all of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nTherefore a configuration satisfying the covering condition cannot contain a sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine, for each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{s}:\\; y=-x+s .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_{s}\\) has slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  Then \\(a+b\\) is an integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), and the point satisfies \\(b=-a+s\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{s}\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines exists. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem\n\nFor a given 