lowed directions (vertical, horizontal, slope \\(-1\\)) with an arbitrary number of additional \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 directions while still covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). A complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\) for general \\(n\\) therefore remains open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{s}:\\; y=-x+s .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(\\ell_{s}\\) have slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  Then \\(a+b\\) is an integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), and the equality \\(b=-a+s\\) shows that \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{s}\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.  Thus a configuration with exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A configuration with one sunny line for \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) consider the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:\\; y=x,\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}:\\; x=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{3}:\\; y=1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_{1}\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is therefore sunny; \\(\\ell_{2}\\) and \\(\\ell_{3}\\) are vertical and horizontal respectively, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nThe required set of points is  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows  \n\n* \\((1,1)\\in\\ell_{1}\\cap\\ell_{2}\\cap\\ell_{3}\\);\n* \\((1,2),(1,3)\\in\\ell_{2}\\);\n* \\((2,1),(3,1)\\in\\ell_{3}\\);\n* \\((2,2)\\in\\ell_{1}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and exactly one of the lines is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remarks on the General Case \\(k\\ge 1\\)\n\nThe original attempt to prove that no sunny line can appear relied on the (incorrect) claim that a point which lies on a vertical line must also lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\).  The covering condition only demands that each point be incident to *some* line of the family; it does **not** require multiple incidences.  Consequently the argument that forces the existence of a slope \\(-1\\) line in the presence of a sunny line collapses.\n\nThe problem of determining all admissible values of \\(k\\) for arbitrary \\(n\\) therefore reduces to the following combinatorial question:\n\n> Given \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(k\\) have slopes different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\), can the family cover the set \\(T_{n}\\)?\n\nThe construction of Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is always possible, while Lemma\u202f2 shows that \\(k=1\\) is possible for \\(n=3\\).  For larger \\(n\\) the existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge 1\\) remains an open problem; no general method or obstruction has been established in the present work. \u220e"

