            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Counting the interior points\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-3\\) solutions with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=4}^{n+1}(s-3)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:x=1,\\qquad \\ell_{2}:y=1,\\qquad \\ell_{3}:x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese three lines cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie on the border of the triangle:\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a=1\\}\\cup\\{(a,b)\\mid b=1\\}\\cup\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points are exactly the set \\(S\\) defined in (1).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nBecause \\(|S|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\ge k\\), we can choose a subset \\(U\\subseteq S\\) with \\(|U|=k\\).\n\nFor each \\((a,b)\\in U\\) choose a rational number \\(\\alpha_{a,b}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{a,b}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\alpha_{a,b}\\neq\\alpha_{c,d}\\ \\text{if }(a,b)\\neq(c,d).\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{(a,b)}:\\; y-b=\\alpha_{a,b}(x-a).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\) and, by construction, has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \nDistinct points give distinct slopes, hence the lines \\(\\{\\ell_{(a,b)}\\mid (a,b)\\in U\\}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{(a,b)}\\mid (a,b)\\in U\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) contains \\(3+k\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\nIf \\(k=n-2\\) then \\(|\\mathcal L_{k}|=n\\) and we are done.\n\nIf \\(k<n-2\\) we need to add further lines to reach a total of \\(n\\).  \nAdd \\(n-(3+k)\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=c_{1},\\;x=c_{2},\\dots ,x=c_{n-(3+k)},\n\\]\n\nwhere the integers \\(c_{i}\\) are chosen distinct from each other and from \\(1\\).  \nVertical lines are non‑sunny, so the number of sunny lines stays equal to \\(k\\).  \n\nAll added vertical lines together with \\(\\ell_{1}\\) cover every point of \\(T_{n}\\) whose first coordinate belongs to the set \\(\\{1,c_{1},\\dots ,c_{n-(3+k)}\\}\\).  \nBecause the set of first coordinates \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) has size \\(n\\) and we have exactly \\(n-(k+2)\\) distinct vertical lines (including \\(\\ell_{1}\\)), every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) appears as the \\(x\\)-coordinate of at least one of these vertical lines.  \nHence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is on a vertical line, on \\(\\ell_{2}\\), on \\(\\ell_{3}\\), or on one of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{(a,b)}\\).  \n\nThus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  At least