\[\n\\alpha_{a}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_{a}\\neq\\alpha_{b}\\;(a\\neq b).\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{a}:\\; y-(n+1-a)=\\alpha_{a}\\bigl(x-a\\bigr).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4) passes through \\(P_{a}\\) and has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{a}\\) is sunny. Distinct \\(a\\)\u2019s give distinct slopes, so the lines \\(\\ell_{a}\\;(a\\in U)\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\{\\ell_{a}\\mid a\\in V\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{a}\\mid a\\in U\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely the \\(k\\) lines indexed by \\(U\\) are sunny.\n\n#### Coverage proof\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\in V\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell_{a}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\in U\\)*, we claim that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a}\\).  \nIndeed, from (4) we have  \n\n\\[\ny = \\alpha_{a}x +\\bigl[(n+1-a)-\\alpha_{a}a\\bigr].\n\\]\n\nBecause the line was chosen to pass through the two points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\), the right\u2011hand side equals \\(b\\) for every integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\). Consequently every point of the column \\(x=a\\) that belongs to \\(T_{n}\\) is on \\(\\ell_{a}\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessity of at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains at most one non\u2011sunny line.\n\n*If the unique non\u2011sunny line is vertical* \\(x=c\\).  \nThe points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b=2,\\dots ,n\\) are not on this line.  \nTwo distinct points \\((1,b_{1})\\) and \\((1,b_{2})\\) cannot lie on a sunny line, because any line through them would be horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\). Hence each of the \\(n-1\\) points \\((1,b