number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. All\u2011non\u2011sunny configuration (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\;x+y=n+1\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe first \\(n-1\\) lines are vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), the last line has slope \\(-1\\); therefore every line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) is non\u2011sunny.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal N\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b=1\\) and consequently \\(a+b=n+1\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. All\u2011sunny configuration (\\(k=n\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1,\\;x+y=2\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach of the first \\(n-1\\) lines is horizontal (slope \\(0\\)), which is *not* allowed for a sunny line. To obtain a sunny family we modify them slightly: for each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) replace the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y=j+\\varepsilon_j,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\varepsilon_j\\) is a non\u2011zero real number chosen so that the slope of \\(S_j\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\). This can be achieved, for instance, by taking  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y=j+\\tfrac12 x .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(\\tfrac12\\), which is sunny. The last line \\(x+y=2\\) has slope \\(-1\\); to make it sunny we replace it by  \n\n\\[\nS_n:\\; x+y=2+\\delta,\n\\]\n\nwith a small non\u2011zero \\(\\delta\\); its slope remains \\(-1\\), so we must tilt it further, e.g.  \n\n\\[\nS_n:\\; y = -x + 2 + \\tfrac13 x = -\\tfrac23 x +2,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-\\tfrac23\\neq