 suitable infinitesimal perturbation of each of the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\). Consequently a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. No mixed configurations (\\(0<k<n\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains both a sunny line \\(L_s\\) and a non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{ns}\\).\n\nEvery non\u2011sunny line belongs to one of the three families:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(y=c\\);\n* diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nConsider the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(L_{ns}\\). Because of the triangular shape of \\(T_n\\), each such line contains at least one point that does **not** belong to any other line of the same family:\n\n* If \\(L_{ns}\\) is vertical, say \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\), then the point \\((c,1)\\) is on \\(L_{ns}\\) but on no other vertical line and on no diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n* If \\(L_{ns}\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\), then \\((1,c)\\) is unique to that line.\n* If \\(L_{ns}\\) is the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\), the unique point is \\((n,1)\\).\n\nCall such a point a **private point** of \\(L_{ns}\\). By definition a private point is covered by \\(L_{ns}\\) and by no other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that is non\u2011sunny.\n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). Any two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) determine a line; if that line were sunny it could contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise the slope would have to be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)). Consequently a single sunny line can cover at most one private point of a given non\u2011sunny line.\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most \\(k\\) sunny lines, at most \\(k\\) private points can be covere