}\\) we have the vertical line  \n     \\[\n     V_i:\\;x=i .\n     \\]\n     Both \\(V_{n-1}:x=n-1\\) and the diagonal  \n     \\[\n     D:\\;x+y=n+1\n     \\]\n     are also non\u2011sunny (slopes \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) respectively).  \n\n   - **Sunny replacements.**  \n     For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) define the line of slope \\(+1\\) passing through the two lattice points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\):\n     \\[\n     S_i:\\;y=x-i+1 .\n     \\]\n     Its slope is \\(+1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n     The set of integer points of \\(S_i\\) inside the triangle is precisely  \n     \\[\n     \\{(i+r,\\,1+r)\\mid r=0,1,\\dots ,n-i-1\\},\n     \\]\n     i.e. the whole \u201cdiagonal\u2019\u2019 that starts at the bottom of column \\(i\\) and proceeds one step up\u2011right until it leaves \\(T_n\\).\n\n   - **Choosing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n     Let \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) be any subset with \\(|I|=k\\).  \n     Construct the family  \n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L(I)=\\bigl\\{\\,S_i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\bigr\\}\n                 \\cup\\bigl\\{\\,V_i\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\setminus I\\,\\bigr\\}\n                 \\cup\\{V_{\\,n-1},\\,D\\}.\n     \\]\n     The number of lines is\n     \\[\n     |I|+(n-2-|I|)+1+1=n,\n     \\]\n     and exactly \\(|I|=k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n4. **Rigorous verification that \\(\\mathcal L(I)\\) covers \\(T_n\\).**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n-a+1\\).  \n   We distinguish two cases.\n\n   *Case 1 \u2013 \\(a\\le n-2\\).*  \n\n   - If \\(a\\in I\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\).  \n     Indeed, by definition of \\(S_a\\) we have the equality\n     \\[\n     b=1+(b-1)=1+(a+b- a-1)=1+(a+b-a-1)=1+(b-1),\n     \\]\n     and the point \\((a,b)\\) can be written as \\((a+r,1+r)\\) with \\(r=b-1\\).  \n     Since \\(b\\le n-a+1\\), we have \\(r=b-1\\le n-a\\), i.e. \\(r\\) is within the allowed range for points