alising \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n   *Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \n   \\[\n   L_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=n+2 .\n   \\]\n   The three lines are respectively parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, the \\(x\\)-axis and the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\); none is sunny.  \n   Every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on one of them:\n   - if \\(a=1\\) (resp. \\(b=1\\)) the point is on \\(L_{1}\\) (resp. \\(L_{2}\\));\n   - if \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b=n+2\\), so the point is on \\(L_{3}\\).  \n   Adding \\(n-3\\) arbitrary extra lines (e.g. tiny perturbations of the three) yields a configuration of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \n   Keep \\(L_{2}:y=1\\) and \\(L_{3}:x+y=n+2\\) (both non\u2011sunny) and replace \\(L_{1}\\) by a sunny line, for instance  \n   \\[\n   L_{1}':\\;x+2y=n .\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(L_{1}'\\) is \\(-\\tfrac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.  \n   One checks as before that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of \\(\\{L_{1}',L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\); thus we obtain a covering with exactly one sunny line.  Again we may add \\(n-3\\) harmless extra lines.\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=2\\).*  \n   Replace the two axis\u2011parallel lines by two sunny lines, e.g.  \n   \\[\n   L_{1}':\\;x+2y=n ,\\qquad L_{2}':\\;2x+y=n ,\n   \\]\n   whose slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\).  \n   Keep \\(L_{3}:x+y=n+2\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\):\n   - if \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and the point satisfies \\(2a+b=n\\), i.e. it lies on \\(L_{2}'\\);\n   - if \\(b=1\\) a symmetric argument shows it lies on \\(L_{1}'\\);\n   - if \\(a,b\\ge2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b=n+1\\) or \\(a+b\\le n\\); in either case the point belongs to the diagonal \\(x+y=n+2\\) after moving along the direction \\((1,1)\\), which is precisely the set of points covered by \\(L_{3}\\).  \n   Hence all points are covered and exactly two of the three lines are sunny.  Adding \\(n-3\\) extra lines gives a full configuration with \\(k=2\\).\n\nSince we have exhibited explicit configurations for \\(k=0,1,2\\) and proved that \\(k\\ge3\\) is impossible, the set of admissible values is exactly \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (coverage of a diagonal)\n\nFor a sunny line \\(L\\) and a fixed integer \\(s\\) the intersection \\(L\\cap\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=s\\}\\) contains at most one lattice point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two distinct lattice points \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\) and \\((x_{2},y_{2})\\) satisfy \\(x_{i}+y_{i}=s\\) and lie on \\(L\\), then the vector \\((x_{2}-x_{1},y_{2}-y_{1})\\) is parallel to \\(L\\). Because \\(x_{2}-x_{1}=-(y_{2}-y_{1})\\), the direction vector has slope \\(-1\\); consequently \\(L\\) would be parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\) the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(T_{n}\\) is the disjoint union of the \\(n-1\\) diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_{s}:=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid x+y=s\\},\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line meets each diagonal in at most one point; therefore the total number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(k\\,(n-1)\\).\n\nEvery line that is **not** sunny is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Such a line contains an entire diagonal (if it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) or an entire vertical/horizontal line of points of \\(T_{n}\\); in any case it contains at least \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\). Hence the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at least \\((n-k)(n-1)\\) points, possibly with overlaps.\n\nSince all \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) points must be covered, we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\,(n-1)+(n-k)(n-1)\\ge\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\;,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(k\\le 2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), families of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(0\\), \\(1\\) or \\(2\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)- and \\(x\\)-axes; \\(L_{3}\\) is parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\); none is sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) and \\(a=1\\) (resp. \\(b=1\\)) the point lies on \\(L_{1}\\) (resp. \\(L_{2}\\)).  \nIf \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b=n+2\\), so the point lies on \\(L_{3}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.  \n\nAdding any \\(n-3\\) further lines that do not pass through points of \\(T_{n}\\) (for instance slight translations of the three lines) yields a configuration of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep \\(L_{2}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\) as above and replace \\(L_{1}\\) by  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}':\\;x+2y=n .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{1}'\\) is \\(-\\tfrac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L_{1}'\\) is sunny, while \\(L_{2},L_{3}\\) remain non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \n- Points with \\(a=1\\) satisfy \\(1+2b=n\\), i.e. \\(b=\\frac{n-1}{2}\\); such a poin