se all those lines have slopes\n\\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  After removing \\(L\\) the point \\(P\\) would be uncovered,\nbut the newly added line \\(L'\\) contains \\(P\\), so every point of \\(S_n\\) is\nstill covered.  \u220e\n\nApplying the Replacement Lemma to the \\(k=0\\) configuration, we can replace a\nnon\u2011sunny line by a sunny one, thereby increasing the number of sunny lines by\nexactly one while preserving the covering property.  Repeating this operation\n\\(k\\) times yields a family with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, provided that after\neach step there remains at least one non\u2011sunny line to replace.  Starting from\n\\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines we can perform the operation up to \\(n-2\\) times, giving\n\\(k\\le n\\).  Since the lemma requires only the existence of *some* non\u2011sunny\nline, we may stop as soon as we have obtained the desired number \\(k\\).  Thus\nfor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of a single sunny line\n\nThe argument of \u00a71 showed that any covering family must contain at least two\nsunny lines.  Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\{0\\}\\cup\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\le n\\) are possible except \\(k=1\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ 2\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]"
