 the integer parameter \\(t\\) can assume at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   different values, and consequently a sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **How many points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a non‑sunny line?**  \n   A non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\n   * vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n   * horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n   * slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n   For each of these families the number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a single line\n   is at most \\(n\\) (the longest possible column, row or diagonal of the\n   triangle).  \n\n   Hence every non‑sunny line contributes **at most \\(n\\) new points** of\n   \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Necessary inequality for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.  \n   Using the bounds from (2) and the previous paragraph, the total number of\n   *distinct* points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   kM+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since all points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   kM+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and treat the two parity\n   cases.\n\n   *If \\(n=2m\\) is even*, then \\(M=m\\).  Inequality (4) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   km+(2m-k)2m\\ge m(2m+1)\n   \\Longrightarrow 4m^{2}-km\\ge 2m^{2}+m\n   \\Longrightarrow k\\le 2m-1=\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd*, then \\(M=m+1\\).  Inequality (4) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   k(m+1)+(2m+1-k)(2m+1)\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Longrightarrow (2m+1)^{2}-k m\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\n   \\Longrightarrow k\\le m=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   In both cases we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Of course \\(k\\ge0\\), so any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining every integer \\(k\\) in the allowed range.**  \n\n   *Base case \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   They are all non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line\n   \\(x=a\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n   *General case \\(1\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).*  \n\n   – **Sunny lines.** For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = x + i .\n   \\]\n\n   Their slope is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore\n   they are sunny.  The line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains the points\n   \\((a,a+i)\\) with \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+(a+i)\\le n+1\\), i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-i}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\ell_i\\) covers exactly\n   \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-i}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   – **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+1\\le c\\le n\\) take the vertical line\n   \\(x=c\\).  There are \\(n-k\\) such lines; they are distinct from the sunny\n   diagonals and are non‑sunny.\n\n   – **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(b-a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny diagonal\n   \\(\\ell_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\n   *If \\(b-a\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)*, then \\(a\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); otherwise\n   \\(b-a\\le k\\).  Hence \\(a\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the vertical line\n   \\(x=a\\) (one of the non‑sunny lines) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\n   The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   lines, all distinct.\n\n   Consequently **every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (5) can be realized**.\n\n6. **Conclusion