Because of (2) the integer \\(|p+q|\\) is at least \\(1\\).  \n   The three families of forbidden directions correspond to the three\n   possibilities \\(|p+q|=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), \\(|p|=0\\) (horizontal) and \\(|q|=0\\) (vertical).\n\n   For a point of \\(S_n\\) we have \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\).  \n   Using (3) we see that as \\(t\\) varies, the quantity \\(x+y\\) changes by the\n   fixed step \\(|p+q|\\).  Consequently the number of admissible integer values\n   of \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|p+q|}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Big\\rfloor+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   At the same time the \\(x\\)-coordinate must stay in \\([1,n]\\).  From (1)\n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x_0+qt\\le n\\Longrightarrow\n   \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n   \\]\n\n   possible values of \\(t\\).  The same bound holds with \\(|p|\\) in place of\n   \\(|q|\\) from the condition on \\(y\\).\n\n   Therefore the number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can lie on a sunny line is\n   bounded by  \n\n   \\[\n   N_{\\text{sunny}}\\le\n   \\min\\Bigl\\{\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Big\\rfloor+1,\\;\n                \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p|}\\Big\\rfloor+1,\\;\n                \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\Big\\rfloor+1\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because of (2) we have \\(|p|\\ge1,\\;|q|\\ge1\\) and \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) **unless**\n   \\((p,q)=(\\pm1,\\pm1)\\).  The case \\((p,q)=(\\pm1,\\pm1)\\) gives slope \\(1\\) (or\n   \\(-1\\) after scaling), which is sunny because it is not parallel to\n   \\(x+y=0\\).  For \\((p,q)=(\\pm1,\\pm1)\\) we have \\(|p+q|=2\\); inserting into (5)\n   yields  \n\n   \\[\n   N_{\\text{sunny}}\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   For every other admissible pair \\((p,q)\\) we have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\) or\n   \\(|p|\\ge2\\) or \\(|q|\\ge2\\), and (5) gives an even smaller bound.  Hence the\n   universal upper bound for a sunny line is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{M:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor } .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n3. **How many points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a non‑sunny line?**  \n   A non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\n   * vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n   * horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n   * slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n   In each family the longest possible intersection with the triangle\n   \\(S_n\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points (the longest column, row or diagonal).\n   Thus any non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) new points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Necessary inequality for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n   Using the bounds from (7) and the previous paragraph, the total number of\n   *distinct* points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   kM+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Since every point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n   \\[\n   kM+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(M=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and treat the two parity\n   cases.\n\n   *If \\(n=2m\\) (even)*, then \\(M=m\\).  Inequality (9) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   km+(2m-k)2m\\ge m(2m+1)\n   \\Longrightarrow 4m^{2}-km\\ge 2m^{2}+m\n   \\Longrightarrow k\\le 2m-1=\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd)*, then \\(M=m+1\\).  Inequality (9) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   k(m+1)+(2m+1-k)(2m+1)\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Longrightarrow (2m+1)^{2}-km\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\n   \\Longrightarrow k\\le m=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   In both cases we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining every integer \\(k\\) in the allowed range.**  \n\n   *Base case \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \