+y\\le n+1\\), the number of\n   admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   N_1=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Big\\rfloor+1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The conditions \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le y\\le n\\) give the further bounds  \n\n   \\[\n   N_2=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\Big\\rfloor+1,\\qquad\n   N_3=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p|}\\Big\\rfloor+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   N_{\\text{sunny}}\\le\\min\\{N_1,N_2,N_3\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   **Lemma 1.** For any integers \\(p,q\\) satisfying the sunny conditions,\n   \\[\n   \\min\\{N_1,N_2,N_3\\}\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.* Because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), at least one of \\(|p|,|q|\\) equals 1.\n\n   *Case 1:* \\(|p|=|q|=1\\).  \n   Then \\(p+q=\\pm2\\) (the case \\(p+q=0\\) is excluded).  \n   Using (1)–(3) we obtain  \n   \\(N_{\\text{sunny}}\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor+1\n   =\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *Case 2:* \\(|p|=1,\\;|q|\\ge2\\).  \n   Here \\(|p+q|\\ge|\\,|q|-|p|\\,|=|q|-1\\ge1\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   N_1\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|-1}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n        \\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n        =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(N_{\\text{sunny}}\\le N_1\\), (4) follows.\n\n   *Case 3:* \\(|q|=1,\\;|p|\\ge2\\) is symmetric to Case 2.\n\n   Thus (4) holds in every admissible situation. ∎\n\n   From Lemma 1 we obtain the uniform bound  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{M:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   for the number of points of \\(S_n\\) that any sunny line can cover.\n\n3. **How many points can a non‑sunny line cover?**  \n   A non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n   In each family the longest possible intersection with the triangle \\(S_n\\)\n   contains at most \\(n\\) lattice points (a whole column, a whole row, or a\n   whole diagonal).  Hence any non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) new\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Necessary condition for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n   Using the bounds above, the total number of *distinct* points of \\(S_n\\)\n   that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   kM+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Because every point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n   \\[\n   kM+(n-k)n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(M\\) from (5) and treating the parity of \\(n\\) gives\n\n   *If \\(n=2m\\) (even):* \\(M=m\\).  Inequality (7) becomes  \n   \\(km+(2m-k)2m\\ge m(2m+1)\\), i.e. \\(k\\le 2m-1=\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).\n\n   *If \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd):* \\(M=m+1\\).  Inequality (7) becomes  \n   \\(k(m+1)+(2m+1-k)(2m+1)\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\\), i.e. \\(k\\le m=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   Hence a **necessary condition** is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0\\le k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor } .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction that attains every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = x + i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   (these are sunny, slope \\(1\\)).  \n   For the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines we take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x = k+j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in (9)–(10) are distinct; the total number is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\n   **Coverage proof.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(b-a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line\n   \\(L_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\n   *Otherwise* \\(b-a\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \n   Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\), the inequality \\(b-a\\notin[1,k]\\) forces\n   \\(a\\ge k+1\\) (otherwise \\(b\\le a+k\\) would give \\(b-a\\le k\\)).  \n   Hence \\(a=k+j\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), and the vertical line\n   \\(V_j\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Thus **every** point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\n   The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, proving that every integer\n   \\(k\\) satisfying (8) is attainable.\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   The necessary condition (8) and the explicit construction together show that\n   the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\, } .\n   \\]\n\n   This completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
