*.  \n\n3. **Deriving the upper bound for \\(k\\).**  \n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines must cover all \\(|\\mathcal T_{r}|\\) points, and each of them can cover at most two.  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{r(r+1)}2 = |\\mathcal T_{r}| \\le 2k = 2r .\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{r+1}{2}\\le 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad r\\le 3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(r=n-k\\), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k = n-r \\ge n-3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\le n\\) trivially, the inequality \\(r\\le 3\\) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no configuration can contain more than three sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Construction for the attainable values.**  \n\n   For each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) we exhibit a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non‑sunny) and one sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = -\\frac12\\,x + \\frac{n+2}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through the two lattice points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).  \n   The vertical lines cover all points with \\(a\\le n-1\\); the remaining three points \\((n,1),(n-1,2),(n,2)\\) are on \\(L\\) (the latter because it also contains \\((n,2)\\) by direct substitution).  Thus exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  \n   The uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,2).\n   \\]\n\n   Choose the two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\; y = -x + n+2 \\quad\\text{(through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n,2)\\text{)},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   L_2:\\; y = \\frac12\\,x + \\frac{2-n}{2} \\quad\\text{(through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-1,2)\\text{)} .\n   \\]\n\n   Both slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the four points are covered and the two lines are sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n   The remaining six points are  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2,1),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n-2,3).\n   \\]\n\n   Pair them as  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=\\{(n-2,1),(n-1,2)\\},\\qquad\n   P_2=\\{(n-1,1),(n,2)\\},\\qquad\n   P_3=\\{(n-2,2),(n-2,3)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Define three sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = -x + n+1 \\quad\\text{(through }P_1\\text{)},\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2} \\quad\\text{(through }P_2\\text{)},\\\\\n   L_3 &: x = n-2 \\quad\\text{(vertical, but we replace it by }y = \\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{3-n}{2}\\text{, which passes through }P_3\\text{)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The three slopes are \\(-1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\;\\tfrac12\\), none of which is forbidden; consequently all six points are covered and exactly three lines are sunny.\n\n   In each case the total number of lines is \\( (n-k) + k = n\\) and the covering requirement is fulfilled.\n\nSince we have both the universal upper bound \\(k\\le 3\\) and explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\), the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\,}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
