hat a single line can contain  \n\n*If the line is sunny.*  \nA sunny line has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nIntersect the strip \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) with the line; the intersection is a segment of length at most \\(n\\).  \nSince the lattice points of \\(A\\) lie on integer coordinates, at most \\(n\\) of them can belong to the line. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap A\\bigr)\\le n\\qquad\\text{for any sunny line }L. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*If the line is non\u2011sunny.*  \n\n- For a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) we have \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n\\) points of \\(A\\) lie on it.\n\n- For a vertical line \\(x=c\\) the same bound holds.\n\n- For a line of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\), the integer points of \\(A\\) on it satisfy \\(c\\le n+1\\).  \n  The possible values of \\(a\\) (and hence of \\(b\\)) range from \\(\\max\\{1,c-n\\}\\) to \\(\\min\\{c-1,n\\}\\); the number of integer solutions is \\(\\lfloor\\frac{c-1}{2}\\rfloor\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap A\\bigr)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\qquad\\text{for any line of type (iii)}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus a non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(A\\).  \nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \nCounting incidences between the lines and the points of \\(A\\) and using (2)\u2013(3) we obtain\n\n\\[\ns\\cdot n + t\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\;\\ge\\;|A|\n   \\;=\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(t=n-s\\) and simplifying gives\n\n\\[\ns\\Bigl(n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr)\n   \\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor =\\frac{n}{2}\\) when \\(n\\) is even and \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) when \\(n\\) is odd, a straightforward case analysis yields\n\n\\[\ns\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + i .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=i+1\\) and \\(a\\ge 1\\); indeed solving (5) for integer \\(a\\) gives \\(b=i+1-a\\). Consequently the union of the \\(L_i\\) covers all points of \\(A\\) whose sum of coordinates is at most \\(m+1\\).\n\n*Vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-m .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach vertical line \\(V_j\\) contains all points of \\(A\\) with abscissa \\(j\\) and ordinate \\(b\\) satisfying \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\).  \n\nNow consider any point \\((a,b)\\in A\\).  \nIf \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(L_{a+b-1}\\).  \nIf \\(a+b\\ge m+2\\) then \\(a\\le n-m\\) (otherwise \\(a\\ge n-m+1\\) would imply \\(a+b\\ge n-m+1+m+2=n+3\\), contradicting \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)). Hence such a point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(A\\) is covered, the lines are distinct, and exactly \\(m\\) of them (the \\(L_i\\)) are sunny. This shows that the lower bound (4) is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Obtaining any larger number of sunny lines  \n\nIf we wish to have \\(k>m\\) sunny lines, start from the construction above and replace any of the vertical lines \\(V_j\\) by a sunny line 