n   - **Line of slope \\(-1\\) (type \\(x+y=c\\)).**  \n     For a fixed \\(c\\le n+1\\) the integer solutions of \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\); there are exactly \\(c-1\\) of them. Hence a line of this type can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(A\\) (attained when \\(c=n+1\\)).  \n\n   Consequently **every** line, sunny or not, can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(A\\). The only improvement for non‑sunny lines is that a line of type \\(x+y=c\\) can contain *fewer* points when \\(c\\) is small; the worst‑case (largest) number is still \\(n\\).\n\n3. **Incidence counting.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   s=\\text{number of sunny lines},\\qquad\n   t=n-s=\\text{number of non‑sunny lines}.\n   \\]\n\n   Counting incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the \\(|A|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points of \\(A\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\cdot n\\;+\\;\\sum_{i=1}^{t} m_i\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   where \\(m_i\\) is the number of points of \\(A\\) lying on the \\(i\\)-th non‑sunny line.\n\n   For a non‑sunny line of type \\(x+y=c\\) we have the sharper bound  \n   \\[\n   m_i\\le c-1\\le n-1,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   because \\(c\\le n+1\\).  \n   For horizontal or vertical non‑sunny lines the bound \\(m_i\\le n\\) is already optimal.\n\n   To obtain a **universal** lower bound on \\(s\\) we may replace each \\(m_i\\) by the *largest* possible value that a non‑sunny line can attain, namely \\(n\\), **except** that at least one of the non‑sunny lines (if any) must be of slope \\(-1\\) (otherwise we would have only horizontal and vertical lines, which would require at least \\(2n-1\\) lines to cover all points, contradicting the hypothesis \\(n\\) lines). Hence we can assume that among the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines there is at most one line of type \\(x+y=c\\); the remaining \\(t-1\\) non‑sunny lines are horizontal or vertical and can each cover at most \\(n\\) points, while the possible slope‑\\(-1\\) line covers at most \\(n-1\\) points.\n\n   Substituting these maximal contributions into (1) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\cdot n\\;+\\;(t-1)\\,n\\;+\\;(n-1)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(t=n-s\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   s n + (n-s-1)n + (n-1) \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\]\n   which simplifies to  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1) \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} - n^{2} = -\\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   s \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n-2}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(s\\) is an integer,  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(s\\) is exactly the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, (3) is the desired necessary condition.\n\n4. **Result.**  \n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(A\\) must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n   The converse – that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized – has not been established; constructing such configurations (or proving a tighter upper bound) remains an open part of the problem. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
