nd \\(M_{n-1}\\), etc.  \n   In particular, the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_{0}:=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\cup\\{M_{1}\\}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (the \\(n\\) vertical lines together with the single horizontal line \\(y=1\\)) already covers \\(A\\).  \n   All lines in \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) are non‑sunny, so \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) realises the case \\(k=0\\) **if** it were allowed; however the problem requires \\(k\\ge\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\), and indeed \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) uses \\(n\\) lines, so it is a valid starting configuration.\n\n   *Step 4.2 – turning a non‑sunny line into a sunny one.*  \n   Replace a vertical line \\(x=i\\) by a sunny line that still passes through all points of \\(A\\) that were previously covered by \\(x=i\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\;y = (i-1)\\,x - (i-1)^{2}+i .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   One checks directly that for every integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\) the point \\((i,b)\\) satisfies (3); indeed\n\n   \\[\n   b = (i-1)i - (i-1)^{2}+i = i .\n   \\]\n\n   More systematically, the set of integer solutions of (3) with \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the same set covered by the vertical line \\(x=i\\).  \n   Moreover the slope of \\(S_{i}\\) equals \\(i-1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for every \\(i\\ge 2\\); for \\(i=1\\) the line \\(S_{1}\\) is simply \\(y=1\\), which is horizontal and therefore non‑sunny – we will never replace \\(x=1\\) by \\(S_{1}\\).\n\n   Consequently, for any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we may replace the vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\) by the sunny lines \\(\\{S_{i}\\mid i\\in I\\}\\) and keep all other lines of \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) unchanged.  \n   The resulting family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines (the slopes \\(i-1\\) are all different) and still covers every point of \\(A\\), because each point \\((i,b)\\) is now