eover, the intersection of \\(S_a\\) with the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is unique, so the only point of column \\(a\\) that lies on \\(S_a\\) is \\((a,1)\\).  If \\(b=1\\) we are done.  If \\(b>1\\) we use the following observation: for any \\(a\\le k\\) the line \\(S_{a+1}\\) (which is also in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)) meets column \\(a\\) at the point\n   \\[\n   (a,\\;(a+1)a+1-(a+1)^{2})=(a,\\;b')\n   \\]\n   where\n   \\[\n   b'=a^{2}+a+1-a^{2}-2a-1= -a .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(-a\\) is not a positive integer, this does not help.  Instead we proceed differently.\n\n   Observe that for each \\(a\\le k\\) the set of admissible \\(b\\) in column \\(a\\) is the interval\n   \\[\n   \\{1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\}.\n   \\]\n   The line \\(S_{k+1}\\) (which is vertical when \\(k=n\\) and otherwise does not belong to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)) is not needed.  The crucial point is that **every** point of column \\(a\\) with \\(b>1\\) also belongs to the vertical line \\(V_{b}\\) (because its \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(a\\) and its \\(y\\)-coordinate equals \\(b\\)).  Since \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\), the vertical line \\(V_{b}\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) whenever \\(b\\ge k+1\\).  Thus:\n\n   * if \\(b\\ge k+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{b}\\in\\mathcal L_k\\);\n   * if \\(b\\le k\\) then \\(b\\le k\\le a\\) (because \\(a\\ge b\\) in the triangle \\(A_n\\)), and consequently the point \\((b, a)\\) belongs to column \\(b\\) with ordinate \\(a\\).  Since \\(a\\le n+1-b\\), the point \\((b,a)\\) is in \\(A_n\\) and, because \\(b\\le k\\), the sunny line \\(S_{b}\\) is in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  The line \\(S_{b}\\) contains \\((b,1)\\); its slope is \\(b\\), so moving one unit to the right raises the ordinate by \\(b\\).  Repeating this step \\(a-1\\) times we obtain exactly the point \\((a,b)\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{b}\\).\n\n   In all cases \\((a,b)\\) is covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n   *If \\(a>k\\)*, then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and clearly contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Therefore every point of \\(A_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n4. **Upper bound.**  \n   Any configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  The construction above shows that the bound \\(k\\le n\\) is attainable for every \\(k\\) in this range.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(A_n\\); and we have proved that no configuration can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
