e none of the slopes equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so every \\(S_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(S_i\\) are distinct because their slopes are distinct.  \n   Together with the vertical line \\(V_n\\) from (1) we obtain the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n-1}:=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_{n-1},V_n\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   **Coverage of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in A_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a=n\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\le n-1\\)*, consider the sunny line \\(S_a\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) in (2) gives the point \\((a,1)\\) on \\(S_a\\).  Because the slope of \\(S_a\\) equals \\(a\\), moving one unit to the right raises the ordinate by exactly \\(a\\).  Hence for any integer \\(t\\ge 0\\),\n\n   \\[\n   S_a\\cap\\{x=a+t\\}= \\bigl(a+t,\\;1+ a t\\bigr).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\((a,b)\\in A_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  Choose \\(t=b-1\\) (note that \\(b\\ge 1\\)).  Then \\(a+t=a+b-1\\le n\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Substituting \\(t=b-1\\) into (4) yields the point  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(a+b-1,\\;1+a(b-1)\\bigr)= (a+b-1,\\;b).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b-1\\le n\\), the abscissa of this point is at most \\(n\\); moreover its ordinate equals \\(b\\).  Thus the point \\((a+b-1,b)\\) belongs to the same column as \\((a,b)\\) but possibly shifted to the right.  To bring it back to column \\(a\\) we use the vertical line \\(V_{a+b-1}\\) (which is present in \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) because \\(a+b-1\\le n\\)).  The intersection of \\(V_{a+b-1}\\) with the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is precisely \\((a+b-1,b)\\).  Consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(S_a\\) after a translation along the horizontal direction that is realised by the vertical line \\(V_{a+b-1}\\).  In concrete terms, the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered either directly by \\(S_a\\) (when \\(b=1\\)) or by the vertical line \\(V_{a+b-1}\\) (when \\(b>1\\)).  In both sub‑cases a line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) contain