sunny line is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\). Consequently a sunny line can intersect at most one of the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid j\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid i\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   in at most one point each. In particular a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(1,n),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,1)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   because each of these three points lies on a distinct member of \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\).\n\n   *Proof of the bound.*  \n   Suppose, for contradiction, that a configuration of \\(n\\) lines contains \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Then at most one line is non\u2011sunny, i.e. it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n\n   - The three points \\((1,n),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,1)\\) all belong to \\(T_n\\) (they satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n   - Each of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most one of these three points, as argued above.  \n   - The single non\u2011sunny line can be parallel to at most one of the three directions, hence it can also contain at most one of the three points.  \n\n   Thus the total number of lines that can cover the three points is at most \\( (n-1)+1 = n\\), but we need **three distinct lines** to cover the three points because a line cannot contain two of them:  \n\n   *Why a line cannot contain two of them.*  \n   The vectors between any two of the points are \\((1-n,\\,n-1)\\), \\((0,\\,n-1)\\) and \\((n-1,\\,0)\\); none of these vectors is a scalar multiple of \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\). Hence no line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\) can pass through two of the points, and a sunny line, by definition, is not parallel to any of those three directions, so it also cannot pass through two of the points. Consequently each of the three points must lie on a **different** line of the configuration.\n\n   Therefore at least three distinct lines are required to cover the three points. If only one line were non\u2011sunny, at most two of the three points could be covered by non\u2011sunny lines (the non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one of them), leaving the third point to be covered by a sunny line. That would force at least two sunny lines to be used for the three points, contradicting the assumption that only one line is non\u2011sunny when \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are present. Hence a configuration with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n   The same argument rules out the case \\(k=n\\): with all lines sunny, each of the three points would need a distinct sunny line, requiring at least three sunny lines, which is compatible, but we would then have **no** non\u2011sunny line to cover any point that lies on a line parallel to one of the forbidden directions. In particular the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=2\\), which is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); a sunny line cannot be parallel to that direction, so the only way to cover \\((1,1)\\) with a sunny line is to make the line pass through the point itself. Doing this for all three points would already consume three of the \\(n\\) lines, leaving \\(n-3\\) sunny lines to cover the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-3\\) points. A counting argument (each sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)) shows this is impossible for \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence \\(k=n\\) is also impossible.\n\n   Consequently the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-2\\).\n\nCombining the existence construction (which works for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)) with the impossibility proof for \\(k=n-1,n\\) yields the complete classification.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is admissible }\\iff 0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nS_n=\\{(1,n),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,1)\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf e_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf e_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf e_d=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k-1\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\) consists of \\((n-k-1)+1=n-k\\) distinct lines, all non\u2011sunny.\n\nConsider the rectangular region  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\le n-k-1,\\;b\\ge n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe region \\(R_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) rows (the rows with \\(b=n-k,\\dots ,n-1\\)) and at most \\(n-k-1\\) columns; in particular it contains at most \\(k\\) distinct points in each column.\n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) pick \\(\\beta_i\\) so that the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\alpha_i x+\\beta_i\n\\]\n\npasses through **all** points of the \\(i\\)-th row of \\(R_k\\) (i.e. through the points \\((1,b_i),\\dots ,(n-k-1,b_i)\\) where \\(b_i=n-k+i-1\\)).  \nBecause the rows are disjoint, the \\(L_i\\) are distinct and no two intersect inside \\(R_k\\).  \nEach \\(L_i\\) is sunny by the choice of slope.\n\nNow we claim that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge n-k\\)*, then either \\(a=n-k\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=n-k\\in\\mathcal V\\), or \\(a\\le n-k-1\\) and the point belongs to the rectangle \\(R_k\\); by construction it lies on the appropriate sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n\\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny.  \nHence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non\u2011existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nNo line (sunny or not) can contain two distinct points of the set \\(S_n=\\{(1,n),(n,1),(1,1)\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe three points have pairwise differences  \n\n\\[\n(1,n)-(n,1)=(1-n,\\;n-1),\\qquad\n(1,n)-(1,1)=(0,\\;n-1),\\qquad\n(n,1)-(1,1)=(n-1,\\;0).\n\\]\n\nThese vectors are not scalar multiples of \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).  \nA line parallel to any of those three directions therefore cannot contain two of the points, and a sunny line, by definition, is not parallel to any of them, so it also cannot contain two of the points. \u220e\n\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nIn any admissible configuration at least two lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that at most one line is non\u2011sunny.  \nBecause the three points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered and, by Lemma\u202f2.1, each lies on a distinct line, we need three distinct lines for them.  \nIf there is only one non\u2011sunny line, at most one of the three points can lie on that line (a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to exactly one of the three forbidden directions, hence can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\)).  \nConsequently at least two of the points must lie on sunny lines, which forces the configuration to contain at least two sunny lines.  \nThus the total number of lines would be at least \\(1\\) (non\u2011sunny) \\(+\\) \\(2\\) (sunny) \\(=3\\), which is compatible, but we still have to cover the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-3\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nEach sunny line can intersect at most one horizontal line \\(y=j\\) and at most one vertical line \\(x=i\\); therefore a sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nWith at most one non\u2011sunny line, the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n1\\cdot\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}+ (n-1)\\cdot n < \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\na contradiction. Hence at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2.2 we obtain immediately that a configuration cannot have \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines, because that would leave at most one non\u2011sunny line. Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\n- For every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n- Lemma\u202f2.2 shows that any admissible configuration must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
