es the equation of this line, i.e. the whole block \\(\\mathcal B\\) can be replaced by the single sunny line without losing any required point.  \nThe replacement reduces the total number of lines by \\(m-1\\) and increases the number of sunny lines by \\(1\\).\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Realising every admissible \\(k\\neq1\\).*  \nStarting from the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines \\(\\{L_t\\}\\) (which gives \\(k=0\\)), we repeatedly apply the replacement of Step\u202f3 with blocks of size \\(2\\).  \nEach such replacement removes exactly one line and adds one sunny line, so after performing the replacement \\(s\\) times we have  \n\n\\[\n\\text{total lines}=n-s,\\qquad \\text{sunny lines}=s .\n\\]\n\nTo bring the total back to \\(n\\) we simply insert \\(s\\) new non\u2011sunny lines of any of the three allowed directions (for instance, distinct vertical lines that do not pass through any already used point).  \nThus we obtain configurations with exactly \\(k=s\\) sunny lines for every integer  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 Impossibility of a single sunny line.*  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly one sunny line \\(S\\).\n\nBecause \\(S\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, it meets each of the three families of parallel non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal, vertical, and slope \\(-1\\)) in at most one point.  \nConsequently \\(S\\) can contain **at most three** points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nAll remaining points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (at least \\(|\\mathcal P_n|-3\\) of them) must be covered by the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nEach non\u2011sunny line belongs to one of the three directions \\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\).  \nFix a direction, say horizontal.  All horizontal lines are of the form \\(y=c\\); each such line can contain at most \\(\\lfloor n+1-c\\rfloor\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). 