"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines if and only if \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Necessary condition.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \n   A sunny line cannot be horizontal, so it can contain **at most one** of these points.  \n   Consequently, if \\(k\\) sunny lines are used, at least \\(n-k\\) other lines are needed to cover the remaining points of the form \\((a,1)\\).  \n   Any non\u2011sunny line that is not vertical is horizontal and would be sunny, so the only non\u2011sunny lines available are the vertical lines \\(x=a\\).  \n   Hence we must have \\(n-k\\le n\\) and, more importantly, the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b>1\\) can be covered only by vertical lines.  \n   This forces \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   All lines are non\u2011sunny and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Replace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = x-(n-1) .\n   \\]\n   This line has slope \\(1\\) (hence it is sunny) and passes through the point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) cover all other points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).  \n   Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\nSince both values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable and no larger \\(k\\) is possible, the answer set is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are precisely the lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A necessary bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nObserve that the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nall belong to \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) because for each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have \\(a+1\\le n+1\\).\n\nA **sunny** line is defined to be a line that is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx\\text{-axis }(y=0),\\qquad y\\text{-axis }(x=0),\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line cannot be horizontal (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of the form \\((a,1)\\) listed in (1); if it contained two distinct such points it would be horizontal, contradicting the definition of sunny.\n\nTherefore, if a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines, at most \\(k\\) of the points in (1) are covered by sunny lines, and the remaining \\(n-k\\) points must be covered by non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Structure of non\u2011sunny lines that can be used  \n\nA non\u2011sunny line must be parallel either to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n* A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis is horizontal; such a line would be sunny, so it cannot be used as a non\u2011sunny line.\n* A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has slope \\(-1\\); it is also sunny (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-axis), so it cannot be used either.\n* The only remaining possibility for a non\u2011sunny line is a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, i.e. a **vertical** line of the form \\(x=c\\).\n\nThus every non\u2011sunny line in a feasible configuration must be vertical.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Consequence for the points with \\(b>1\\)\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\) with \\(b>1\\).  \nSince the only non\u2011sunny lines are vertical, the only way for \\((a,b)\\) to be covered by a non\u2011sunny line is that the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present in the configuration.\n\nHence, to cover **all** points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) with \\(b>1\\) we must include the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-1\\) distinct vertical lines, each of which is non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nFrom Section\u202f1 we need at least \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines to cover the points \\((a,1)\\) in (1).  \nFrom Section\u202f3 we already must use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines in (2).  \nTherefore the total number of lines in any admissible configuration is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1) + (n-k - (n-1)) = n-k .\n\\]\n\nBut the problem requires **exactly** \\(n\\) lines. Consequently we must have  \n\n\\[\nn \\ge n-k \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain more than one sunny line**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.*  \nThe set contains \\(n-1\\) vertical lines and one horizontal line, so \\(|\\mathcal L_{0}|=n\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny.*  \nAll lines are either vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) or horizontal (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis); therefore none is sunny.\n\n*Covering property.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b=1\\); the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains \\((n,1)\\).\n\nHence every required point lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\).  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Existence for \\(k=1\\)\n\nReplace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y = x-(n-1).\n\\]\n\n*Sunny.*  \n\\(L\\) has slope \\(1\\); it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\)). Hence \\(L\\) is sunny.\n\n*Passes through \\((n,1)\\).*  \nSubstituting \\(x=n\\) gives \\(y=n-(n-1)=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\in L\\).\n\n*All other points.*  \nThe vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) are unchanged, and as argued in Section\u202f3 they cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) with \\(b>1\\).  \nThe point \\((n,1)\\) (the only point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) not covered by a vertical line) is now covered by the sunny line \\(L\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{1}= \\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,L\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly one sunny line and still satisfies the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration can have more than one sunny line (\\(k\\le 1\\)).\n* Configurations exist for \\(k=0\\) and for \\(k=1\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]"

