exactly \\(n+1-a\\) points.\n\nA sunny line meets a column in at most one point.  Consequently, if\n\\(s\\) sunny lines are present, they can together cover at most \\(s\\) points\nin each column.\n\nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets column \\(C_a\\) only when \\(a=c\\); in that case it\ncovers **all** points of the column.  A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets column\n\\(C_a\\) in exactly one point (the point \\((a,c)\\) provided it belongs to\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\)).  A line of slope \\(-1\\) meets column \\(C_a\\) in at most one\npoint as well.\n\nThus, apart from at most one column, every column must be covered by a\nvertical line; otherwise we would need more than \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines to\ncover the remaining points of that column, contradicting the fact that we have\nonly \\(n-s\\) non‑sunny lines in total.\n\nSince there are \\(n\\) columns but only \\(n-s\\) non‑sunny lines, we must have\n\\(n-s\\ge n-1\\); i.e. \\(s\\le 1\\) **unless** one column is covered by a vertical\nline and the remaining columns are covered by the other non‑sunny lines.\nA careful counting of the points in the first two columns\n\\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\) shows that at most two sunny lines can be accommodated.\n(Indeed, column \\(C_1\\) contains \\(n\\) points; if it were not covered by a\nvertical line, at least \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines would be needed, impossible.\nHence \\(x=1\\) must be one of the vertical lines.  Column \\(C_2\\) contains\n\\(n-1\\) points; after using the vertical line \\(x=1\\) we have \\(n-1\\) lines\nleft, of which at most two can be sunny, otherwise the remaining\n\\(n-3\\) non‑sunny lines could not cover the \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_2\\).)\n\nTherefore \\(s\\le 2\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each admissible \\(k\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Construction)\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the following \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Vertical lines } x=1,\\,x=2,\\,\\dots ,\\,x=n-1,\\\\\n&\\text{Horizontal line } y=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are distinct, none is sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal\nP_n\\) lies either on its vertical line \\(x=a\\) (if \\(a\\le n-1\\)) or on the\nhorizontal line \\(y=1\\) (when \\(b=1\\)).  Hence the covering condition holds.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nChoose the sunny line \\(L_1\\colon y=x\\) (slope 1) and the following\n\\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Vertical lines } x=2,\\,x=3,\\,\\dots ,\\,x=n-1,\\\\\n&\\text{Horizontal line } y=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) contains all points \\((a,a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\).\nEvery remaining point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq a\\) either has \\(b=1\\) (hence lies on\n\\(y=1\\)) or has \\(a\\ge 2\\) and therefore lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\nThus all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered and exactly one sunny line is\npresent.\n\n*Case \\(k=2\\).*  \nTake the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1\\colon y=x,\\qquad L_2\\colon y=x+1,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nboth of slope 1, together with the following \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Vertical lines } x=3,\\,x=4,\\,\\dots ,\\,x=n-1,\\\\\n&\\text{Horizontal line } y=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe two sunny lines cover precisely the points whose difference \\(b-a\\) equals\n\\(0\\) or \\(1\\).  For any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(b-a\\notin\\{0,1\\}\\)\nwe have either \\(b=1\\) (hence the point lies on \\(y=1\\)) or \\(a\\ge 3\\) (hence\nthe point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)).  Consequently every point of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered, and exactly two sunny lines appear.\n\nIn each of the three cases the lines a