\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nA line \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\).  Hence it contains \\(n+1-b\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\nThe function \\(b\\mapsto n+1-b\\) attains its maximum at \\(b=1\\), giving\n\\(n\\) points, but the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n\\); thus the\nlargest possible number is obtained when \\(b=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\),\nyielding \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n*Vertical lines* are symmetric and give the same bound.\n\n*Lines of slope \\(-1\\).*  \nA line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) contains the points \\((a,c-a)\\) where\n\\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\).  Hence it contains \\(c-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  The\nmaximum of \\(c-1\\) under the restriction \\(c\\le n+1\\) is attained at\n\\(c=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), giving again\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(\\mathcal{P}\\) and contains exactly \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines, then\n\n\\[\ns\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) by Lemma\u202f1.  The remaining \\(n-s\\) sunny lines can\ncontain at most all the points not already covered, but they cannot increase\nthe left\u2011hand side of (3).  Since every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) must be covered,\nthe total number of points that can be supplied by the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines\nmust be at least \\(|\\mathcal{P}|\\), which is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) by (2). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Consequence  \n\nFrom (3) we obtain\n\n\\[\ns\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}\n               {\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}\n   \\;=\\;\n   \\begin{cases}\n      \\displaystyle\\frac{n}{2}, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[4pt]\n      \\displaystyle\\frac{n+1}{2}, & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer, this is equivalent to\n\n\\[\ns\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(k\\) denotes the number of **sunny** lines, i.e. \\(k=n-s\\).\nTherefore\n\n\\[\nk\\;=\\;n-s\\;\\le\\;n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\;=\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nmust hold for any admissible configuration.\n\n---\n\nThus any admissible value of \\(k\\) necessarily satisfies (4).  The construction of\nfamilies of lines attaining each integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\)\nremains open; the present work establishes only the necessary lower bound on\n\\(k\\). \u220e"
