\\mathcal{P}\\) can be covered by \\(\\displaystyle\nt:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose \\(t\\) distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_{1},\\dots,\\alpha_{t}\\) that are not\n\\(0,\\infty\\) and not \\(-1\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha_{i}=i\\)).  \nProceed inductively: at step \\(i\\) consider the set \\(\\mathcal{P}_{i-1}\\) of\nstill‑uncovered points; pick any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_{i-1}\\) and draw the\nline of slope \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) through it.  By Lemma 1 this line contains at most\n\\(M\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\); therefore after the \\(t\\) steps the total\nnumber of points covered is at most \\(tM\\).  Because\n\\(tM\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\cdot\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|\\mathcal{P}|\\),\nall points are indeed covered.  The lines are distinct because they have\ndifferent slopes. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (realising any \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the \\(t=\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines given by Lemma 3; they already\ncover all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  \n\n*If \\(k=t\\)*, we are done (the remaining \\(n-t\\) lines may be any lines that do\nnot pass through any point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\); they can be taken sunny as well,\nso the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\)).\n\n*If \\(k>t\\)*, add \\(k-t\\) further sunny lines that avoid \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) (choose\ndifferent slopes and intercepts far outside the triangle).  The total number\nof sunny lines becomes \\(k\\); the extra lines do not affect the covering\nproperty.\n\n*If \\(k<n\\)*, we need some non‑sunny lines.  Replace \\(n-k\\) of the “extra”\nlines (those that avoided \\(\\mathcal{P}\\)) by non‑sunny lines (horizontal,\nvertical or slope \\(-1\\)) that also avoid \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  Because they do not\nintersect any required point, the covering of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) remains unchanged.\nThus we obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Result  \n\nLemma 2 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\); Lemma 4 shows that\nevery integer \\(k\\) satisfying this inequality can indeed be achieved.  Hence the\nset of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\bigl\\lceil \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil \\;\\le\\; k \\;\\le\\; n \\; } .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
