cal.  \n   For a fixed \\(j\\) the points of \\(S_n\\) with abscissa \\(j\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=\\{(j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\},\n   \\qquad |P_j|=n+1-j .\n   \\]\n\n   The three non‑sunny directions \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D,\\mathcal V\\) intersect the vertical line \\(x=j\\) in at most one point each; consequently **any** non‑vertical line can meet \\(P_j\\) in at most one point.  \n   Hence, if the vertical line \\(x=j\\) is missing, we need at least \\(|P_j|\\) distinct non‑vertical lines to cover the points of \\(P_j\\).\n\n   Since \\(|P_j|\\ge 2\\) for every \\(j\\le n-1\\), omitting **two** vertical lines would already require at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n+1-j_1)+(n+1-j_2)\\ge 2+(2)=4\n   \\]\n\n   non‑vertical lines, i.e. more than the total number \\(n\\) of lines allowed.  \n   Therefore **at most one** vertical line can be omitted; equivalently at least \\(n-1\\) of the chosen lines must be vertical (or, more generally, belong to the family \\(\\mathcal V\\)).  \n\n   *Step 3.2 – Consequence for sunny lines.*  \n   A sunny line is **not** vertical, horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence every sunny line is non‑vertical, and the previous paragraph shows that there can be **at most one** sunny line among the \\(n\\) lines.  \n   Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Realising the two possibilities.**  \n\n   *Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently the point lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \n   All lines are non‑sunny, so this configuration yields \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   M:\\;y-x=1-n\\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{, not parallel to any forbidden direction)}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   – If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\).  \n   – If \\(a=n\\) then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   1-n = 1-n = y-x,\n   \\]\n\n   hence it lies on \\(M\\).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered, and exactly one line (namely \\(M\\)) is sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument forces \\(k\\le 1\\); the explicit constructions above show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable.  \n   Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
