ny** (i.e. belong to none of the three families).  \n   Fix a vertical line \\(x=j\\) that is **not** present in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (such a line exists because at most \\(n-2\\) vertical lines are used).  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) on this vertical line are  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=\\{(j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-j\\},\\qquad |P_j|=n+1-j\\ge 2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Any line that is not vertical meets the line \\(x=j\\) in at most one point; therefore each point of \\(P_j\\) must be covered by a **different** non‑vertical line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain at least \\(|P_j|\\ge 2\\) non‑vertical lines.\n\n   Perform the same argument for a horizontal line \\(y=i\\) that is missing from \\(\\mathcal L\\) and for a diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\) that is missing.  \n   Each missing line forces the presence of at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that are not parallel to it.  \n   Because the three missing lines are pairwise non‑parallel, the six forced lines are all distinct.  \n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\) would contain at least \\(6\\) lines, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\) with \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\n   Therefore at most one of the three families can be represented by fewer than \\(n-1\\) lines; the other two families together already supply at least \\(n-1\\) lines. ∎\n\n4. **Consequences for sunny lines.**  \n\n   By Lemma 1, at least \\(n-1\\) of the chosen lines belong to a single non‑sunny family.  \n   A sunny line is, by definition, **not** a member of any of the three families, so it cannot be among those \\(n-1\\) lines.  \n   Hence the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-(n-1)=1 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Realising the two possibilities.**  \n\n   *Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); thus the point lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \n   All lines are non