rtical nor horizontal satisfies both conditions of (1) and (2); a line that is vertical (resp. horizontal) satisfies neither.  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is not vertical and meets }P_j\\},\\qquad\n   B=\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is not horizontal and meets }Q_i\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   From (1) and (2) we have \\(|A|\\ge |P_j|\\) and \\(|B|\\ge |Q_i|\\).  \n   The sets \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) may intersect, but any line belonging to the intersection can cover **at most one** point of \\(P_j\\) and **at most one** point of \\(Q_i\\).  \n   Consequently the total number of distinct lines needed to cover all points of \\(P_j\\cup Q_i\\) is at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\max\\{|P_j|,|Q_i|\\}\\;+\\;\\bigl(\\min\\{|P_j|,|Q_i|\\}-\\#\\text{lines that cover one point of each set}\\bigr)\n   \\ge \\max\\{|P_j|,|Q_i|\\}+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since both \\(|P_j|\\) and \\(|Q_i|\\) are at least \\(2\\), we obtain the simple lower bound  \n\n   \\[\n   |A\\cup B|\\ge \\max\\{|P_j|,|Q_i|\\}+1\\ge 3. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   **(iv) Total number of lines.**  \n   The collection \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most \\(n-2\\) vertical lines and at most \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines.  \n   Adding the at least three distinct non‑vertical/non‑horizontal lines required by (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal L|\\ge (n-2)+(n-2)+3 = 2n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(2n-1>n\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).  \n   Hence our assumption that two families each contribute at most \\(n-2\\) lines is impossible.\n\n   The same argument works for the pairs \\((\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D)\\) and \\((\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D)\\): replace “horizontal” by “diagonal” (lines of the form \\(x+y=c\\)); the only change is that a diagonal line meets a missing vertical line in at most one point and meets a missing diagonal line in at most one point, so the counting proceeds identically.\n\n   Therefore at most one of the three families can have fewer than \\(n-1\\) representatives in \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n4. **Bounding the number of sunny lines.**  \n   A sunny line belongs to none of the families \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\).  \n   Lemma 1 forces at least \\(n-1\\) of the \\(n\\) lines to lie in a *single* one of those families; the remaining line (if any) may be outside the families, i.e. sunny.  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-(n-1)=1 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Realising the two possibilities.**  \n\n   *Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); thus the point lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \n   All lines are non‑sunny, so this configuration yields \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   M:\\;y-x=1-n\\qquad\\bigl(\\text{slope }1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   **Verification.**  \n   – If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\).  \n   – If \\(a=n\\) then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   1-n = y-x,\n   \\]\n\n   hence it lies on \\(M\\).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered, and exactly one line (namely \\(M\\)) is sunny.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 forces \\(k\\le 1\\); the explicit constructions above show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable.  \n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instruction