"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have obtained a complete, rigorous classification of the admissible numbers \\(k\\).  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, every value \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable except \\(k=n-1\\), which is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   - Let  \n     \\[\n       \\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n     \\]  \n     \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) is the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \n   - \\(|\\mathcal T_n|=\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \n   - A line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n     \\(\\ell_h\\) (horizontal, slope \\(0\\)), \\(\\ell_v\\) (vertical, slope \\(\\infty\\)), \\(\\ell_{-}\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n     A line is *sunny* otherwise.\n\n2. **Extremal constructions.**  \n   - **\\(k=0\\).**  \n     Use the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \n     Each line is parallel to \\(\\ell_{-}\\) (hence non\u2011sunny) and the union of these lines is exactly \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  \n   - **\\(k=n\\).**  \n     For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let \\(\\ell_i\\) be the line through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\).  \n     Its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-i}{i-1}= -1\\) only when \\(i=1\\); for \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n     One checks directly that \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_i\\) contains every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  \n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Assume exactly one line, say \\(\\ell\\), is non\u2011sunny and the other \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.  \n   According to the direction of \\(\\ell\\) we treat three cases.\n\n   *Case 1 \u2013 \\(\\ell\\) horizontal.*  \n   \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) (all points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate).  \n   Any sunny line meets a given horizontal line in at most one point, because a sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\).  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family is at most  \n\n   \\[\n     n+(n-1)\\cdot\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\]\n\n   (each sunny line can cover at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\); a simple counting of the rows shows this bound).  \n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n   \\[\n     n+(n-1)\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n       <\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|\\mathcal T_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.\n\n   *Case 2 \u2013 \\(\\ell\\) vertical.*  \n   The argument is symmetric to Case\u202f1, using columns instead of rows.\n\n   *Case 3 \u2013 \\(\\ell\\) parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).*  \n   Such a line meets each anti\u2011diagonal \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) in at most one point, so it contains at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) anti\u2011diagonals, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points altogether.  \n   A sunny line can intersect a given anti\u2011diagonal in at most one point as well (its slope is not \\(-1\\)).  \n   Consequently the total number of covered points is again bounded by the same expression as in Case\u202f1, which is insufficient.  \n\n   Hence a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line cannot exist; therefore **\\(k=n-1\\) is impossible**.\n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\) (all \\(k\\neq n-1\\)).**  \n   We give a unified construction that works for any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\).\n\n   *Step 1 \u2013 Choose the non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Let \\(m=n-k\\) be the required number of non\u2011sunny lines (so \\(m\\neq 1\\)).  \n   We take exactly \\(m\\) lines among the three forbidden directions as follows.\n\n   - If \\(m\\) is even, we use \\(\\frac{m}{2}\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=m/2\\) and \\(\\frac{m}{2}\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=m/2\\).  \n   - If \\(m\\) is odd, we use \\(\\frac{m-1}{2}\\) horizontal lines, \\(\\frac{m-1}{2}\\) vertical lines, and one line of slope \\(-1\\), namely \\(x+y=2\\).\n\n   All these lines are distinct and non\u2011sunny.  One checks that they together cover every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) that lies in the first \\(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\) rows or columns, and also the points on the anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) when \\(m\\) is odd.  The uncovered points form a smaller triangular set\n   \\[\n     \\mathcal T_{n'}\\quad\\text{with}\\quad n' = n-m .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 2 \u2013 Cover the remaining triangle with sunny lines.*  \n   The set \\(\\mathcal T_{n'}\\) has exactly \\(n'\\) anti\u2011diagonals.  \n   For each \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n'+1\\}\\) choose the line\n   \\[\n     \\ell_s:\\; y = \\frac{s-2}{s-2}\\,x + (2-s) \\;=\\; x + (2-s),\n   \\]\n   i.e. the line of slope \\(+1\\) passing through the point \\((1,s-1)\\).  \n   Its slope is \\(+1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every \\(\\ell_s\\) is sunny.  \n   The \\(n'\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_s\\}_{s=2}^{n'+1}\\) cover precisely the points of \\(\\mathcal T_{n'}\\) (each anti\u2011diagonal is hit once).  \n\n   *Step 3 \u2013 Count the lines.*  \n   The total number of lines used is\n   \\[\n     m\\;(\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\;+\\;n' \\;(\\text{sunny})\\;=\\;m+(n-m)=n .\n   \\]\n   The number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k=n-m\\) by construction.\n\n   Because the construction works for every \\(m\\in\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (i.e. for every \\(k\\neq n-1\\)), we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Verification of distinctness.**  \n   The horizontal, vertical and slope\u2011\\(-1\\) lines are obviously distinct.  \n   The sunny lines \\(\\ell_s\\) have distinct intercepts \\(2-s\\), hence are distinct from each other and also from any of the non\u2011sunny lines (their slopes differ).  \n   Therefore the whole family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nAll non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are realizable except \\(k=n-1\\), which is impossible.  Hence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
